  {
    "topicTitle": "Dependency Management",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-5-section-1",
        "title": "Dependency Management — Fundamentals and Maven Dependency Model",
        "content": "Maven dependency management is the process of declaring, resolving, organizing, and maintaining the external libraries required by a software project. A Java application rarely contains every capability in its own source code. It commonly relies on libraries for logging, JSON processing, database access, testing, security, web development, and other features. Maven reads dependency declarations from the Project Object Model (POM), identifies the requested artifacts through their coordinates, and resolves the required files from configured repositories. A dependency declaration normally contains groupId, artifactId, and version. Maven uses these values to identify the exact artifact that the project needs. The dependency is then made available to the project build according to its scope and dependency graph. Managing dependencies centrally helps teams avoid manually downloading JAR files, copying libraries into source directories, and maintaining different versions on individual developer machines. A reliable dependency strategy also considers compatibility, security updates, transitive dependencies, repository availability, and reproducible builds. For example, a Spring application may require Spring libraries, a database driver, a logging implementation, and testing libraries. Maven resolves these declared requirements and their required transitive dependencies so the project can compile, test, and package consistently."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-5-section-2",
        "title": "Dependency Management — Declaring Dependencies in pom.xml",
        "content": "Dependencies are normally declared inside the dependencies element of a Maven POM. Each dependency identifies an artifact using its groupId, artifactId, and version, with optional elements such as scope, type, classifier, and exclusions. For example, a project can declare a JSON processing library using groupId com.fasterxml.jackson.core, artifactId jackson-databind, and an explicitly selected version. Maven reads this declaration during project model construction and attempts to resolve the artifact from the available local and remote repositories. The dependency becomes available to the project according to its scope. A dependency should be declared because application code, tests, or build components actually require it, rather than because another project happens to use it. Avoid adding several versions of the same library without understanding the dependency graph, because version conflicts can produce runtime errors such as NoSuchMethodError or ClassNotFoundException. Keep declarations readable and place related dependencies in a consistent order. In team projects, review dependency changes as part of code review so that new libraries, versions, licenses, and security implications are understood before they enter the application."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-5-section-3",
        "title": "Dependency Management — Centralized Versions with dependencyManagement",
        "content": "The dependencyManagement element allows a parent POM or an importing BOM to define dependency information centrally without automatically adding every managed dependency to a child project. This distinction is important: dependencies declares libraries that a project actually uses, while dependencyManagement supplies default configuration such as versions and scopes when those dependencies are declared. A child module can therefore declare a dependency without repeating its version when the version is managed by an applicable parent or imported BOM. This approach is especially useful in multi-module repositories where several services or modules use the same framework libraries. It reduces version drift and makes coordinated upgrades easier. A parent POM can manage versions for commonly used libraries, while each child module chooses which libraries it needs. Maven applies dependency management during model construction and dependency resolution according to inheritance and import rules. Teams should avoid confusing a managed dependency with an automatically included dependency. If a library is only listed under dependencyManagement and is never declared as a dependency in the effective project model, it is not added to the application's classpath merely because it is managed."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-5-section-4",
        "title": "Dependency Management — Importing Bill of Materials (BOM)",
        "content": "A Bill of Materials, commonly called a BOM, is a POM that centralizes compatible versions of a group of related dependencies. Maven projects can import a BOM inside dependencyManagement using type pom and scope import. The imported BOM contributes managed dependency information to the consuming project, helping related libraries remain on a tested and compatible version set. Framework ecosystems often publish BOMs so application teams do not need to choose every component version independently. For example, a Spring Boot application can use the Spring Boot dependency management information to align Spring modules and related libraries. A BOM does not mean that every dependency listed inside it is automatically added to the application. The project still declares the individual dependencies it actually uses. BOM imports are particularly useful when multiple applications share a platform baseline or when a company maintains a common dependency policy. When importing multiple BOMs, inspect the effective POM and understand which managed version wins when definitions overlap. Avoid importing unrelated BOMs without checking compatibility, because conflicting version recommendations can make dependency resolution harder to reason about."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-5-section-5",
        "title": "Dependency Management — Version Selection and Conflict Mediation",
        "content": "A Maven project can reach the same library through multiple dependency paths. One direct dependency may require a particular version while another library brings a different version transitively. Maven constructs a dependency graph and applies dependency mediation rules to select the version used for a given artifact coordinate. A commonly encountered rule is nearest definition: the version found through the path with the fewest dependency levels is selected. When competing versions occur at the same depth, declaration order can influence the result. A direct dependency can therefore be used to make the intended version explicit when a transitive version is unsuitable. Dependency convergence checks from suitable build plugins can help detect inconsistent versions across the graph. Developers should not assume that the newest version is always selected automatically or that every transitive request is included independently. Investigate the actual graph with commands such as mvn dependency:tree and inspect the effective POM when version selection is surprising. Correct conflict resolution requires understanding which component introduced each version and whether the selected version is compatible with all consumers."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-5-section-6",
        "title": "Dependency Management — Exclusions and Optional Dependencies",
        "content": "Sometimes a dependency brings another library that the application does not need, conflicts with an approved implementation, or creates an unwanted runtime component. Maven supports exclusions on a dependency declaration to remove a specified transitive artifact from that dependency path. An exclusion identifies the unwanted artifact by groupId and artifactId. Exclusions should be narrow and evidence-based because removing a library may break the component that expected it. Optional dependencies have a different purpose. When a library declares a dependency as optional, downstream consumers do not automatically inherit it in the same way as an ordinary transitive dependency. The consuming project can still declare that dependency explicitly if it needs the capability. Exclusions are useful when a third-party library brings an unwanted logging implementation or an incompatible transitive component, while optional dependencies allow library authors to expose integrations without forcing every consumer to install them. After applying either mechanism, inspect the dependency tree and run compilation and tests. A successful dependency resolution alone does not prove that the application still has all required runtime classes."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-5-section-7",
        "title": "Dependency Management — Effective POM and Dependency Diagnostics",
        "content": "The effective POM is the Maven project model after inheritance, interpolation, profiles, and dependency management have been applied. It helps explain why a dependency version or configuration appears in the final build even when it is not written directly in the current POM. Run mvn help:effective-pom to inspect the effective model. Use mvn dependency:tree to display direct and transitive dependencies, and use mvn dependency:tree -Dverbose when additional conflict information is needed and supported by the installed plugin version. The command mvn dependency:analyze can help identify dependencies that appear to be used or declared inconsistently, although its findings require human review. When a dependency cannot be resolved, check its coordinates, repository configuration, network access, authentication, and whether the artifact is actually published. When an unexpected version is selected, trace the paths in the dependency tree and inspect dependencyManagement. These diagnostic commands should be run from the correct project directory and with the relevant Maven profiles enabled. Save useful command output in CI logs so that dependency changes can be investigated consistently across developer machines and build agents."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-5-section-8",
        "title": "Dependency Management — Dependency Hygiene, Security, and Upgrade Policy",
        "content": "Dependency management is also a software supply-chain responsibility. Every external library introduces code maintained outside the application team, so dependency choices should be reviewed for maintenance status, known vulnerabilities, licensing, compatibility, and organizational approval. Prefer maintained releases from trusted repositories and avoid unnecessary dependencies that increase the application's attack surface and build complexity. Keep versions explicit or centrally managed rather than relying on accidental version selection. Establish a controlled upgrade process: review release notes, evaluate breaking changes, run unit and integration tests, scan dependencies, and deploy through a suitable validation environment before production rollout. Automated dependency update tools can propose changes, but their pull requests still require testing and review. CI pipelines can enforce policies through vulnerability scanning, license checks, dependency convergence checks, and approved repository controls. Do not treat a clean build as proof that dependencies are secure. Record why important libraries are used and define ownership for critical upgrades. For production services, prioritize vulnerabilities according to exploitability, exposure, available fixes, and business impact rather than blindly upgrading every library without compatibility testing."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-5-section-9",
        "title": "Dependency Management — Multi-Module and Enterprise Dependency Governance",
        "content": "In a multi-module Maven repository, dependency versions should be governed consistently so that modules do not accidentally compile and run against incompatible library combinations. A root parent POM can define shared dependencyManagement entries, while each module declares only the dependencies it actually uses. Platform BOMs can provide a tested baseline for framework components, and organization-wide parent POMs can standardize approved plugin and repository policies where appropriate. Enterprise teams should document ownership of shared versions and define how security fixes are evaluated and rolled out. Avoid copying large dependency lists into every module because duplicated declarations become difficult to maintain. Also avoid putting every possible library into a parent dependencies section if modules do not all need those libraries. Use the effective POM and dependency tree for individual modules to verify the final model. CI should build affected modules and, where practical, run integration tests across module boundaries. When changing a shared version, assess the impact on every consuming module rather than assuming that a successful build of the parent project guarantees runtime compatibility for all applications."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-5-section-10",
        "title": "Dependency Management — Production Hands-on Dependency Investigation",
        "content": "Consider a production Java service whose build begins failing after a library upgrade. The first step is to reproduce the failure with the same commit, JDK, Maven version, settings, and active profiles used by CI. Run mvn dependency:tree and identify which dependency paths introduce the affected library. Run mvn help:effective-pom to verify inherited versions and imported BOMs. Compare the previous successful build with the failing build to isolate the dependency change. Check whether a direct dependency, a transitive dependency, or a managed version caused the selected artifact to change. If a transitive component is unwanted, evaluate a narrow exclusion; if the version must be controlled, consider dependencyManagement or an explicit direct dependency after checking compatibility. Then run clean compilation, unit tests, integration tests, packaging, and the relevant security checks. Confirm that the resulting artifact contains the expected runtime libraries and test the application in a staging environment. Document the root cause, the selected version, the reason for the fix, and any follow-up upgrade work. This process makes dependency changes reviewable and reduces the risk of fixing a build error while introducing a production runtime failure."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "Dependency Scopes",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-6-section-1",
        "title": "Dependency Scopes — Purpose, Classpaths, and Scope Selection",
        "content": "Maven dependency scope defines where a dependency is available during the different stages of a project build. A Java dependency can be needed to compile application source code, execute tests, run the packaged application, or provide an API during compilation while the actual implementation is supplied by the runtime environment. Scope communicates this requirement to Maven and influences the compile classpath, runtime classpath, test classpath, and transitive dependency behavior. Without correct scopes, a project may compile successfully but fail when started because a required library is missing. It may also package libraries that should be supplied by an application server or container, increasing artifact size and creating version conflicts. Maven provides compile, provided, runtime, test, system, and import scope behavior. The import value is special and is used for BOM imports inside dependencyManagement rather than as an ordinary application classpath scope. Selecting a scope requires understanding who supplies the library, which source code needs it, and when the dependency must be available. A production Java service should distinguish libraries required by its application from APIs supplied by its deployment platform and libraries used only by tests."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-6-section-2",
        "title": "Dependency Scopes — Compile Scope and Application Classpaths",
        "content": "Compile is Maven's default dependency scope when no scope is explicitly specified. A compile-scoped dependency is available on the compile, test, and runtime classpaths of the project. It is also transitive by default, meaning downstream projects can receive it through the dependency graph unless another rule changes that behavior. This scope is appropriate when application source code directly uses classes from the dependency and the library must also be available when the application executes. Examples include a library used by service code for JSON processing or a database client library that the application itself must load at runtime. Because compile is the default, an omitted scope is not equivalent to having no scope. Teams should nevertheless make important scope decisions deliberately rather than adding every library without considering its lifecycle role. A compile dependency can increase the runtime dependency set and may introduce version conflicts with other libraries. Inspect the resolved graph with mvn dependency:tree and verify the packaged application when changing compile dependencies. Use compile only when the project genuinely needs the dependency for ordinary application compilation and execution."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-6-section-3",
        "title": "Dependency Scopes — Provided Scope and Container-Supplied APIs",
        "content": "Provided scope is used when a dependency is required to compile and test the project but is expected to be supplied by the runtime environment. Maven makes a provided dependency available on the compile and test classpaths, but not on the ordinary runtime classpath, and it is not transitive to downstream projects in the same way as compile dependencies. A typical example is a Servlet API used by a web application that will be deployed to a compatible application server. The application needs the API classes to compile servlet-related source code, while the server provides the implementation at runtime. Declaring such an API as compile can cause the application to package a duplicate or incompatible copy, depending on the packaging configuration. Declaring it as provided communicates that the deployment platform owns that runtime responsibility. However, provided must not be selected merely because a dependency is large or inconvenient. Confirm that the actual target environment supplies a compatible version. If the application runs as a self-contained executable without an external container supplying the API, provided may be incorrect. Test the artifact in an environment that matches production to detect missing runtime classes."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-6-section-4",
        "title": "Dependency Scopes — Runtime Scope and Execution-Only Libraries",
        "content": "Runtime scope is appropriate when application code does not need the dependency to compile but the application needs it when executing. Maven includes runtime dependencies on the runtime and test classpaths, but excludes them from the main compile classpath. A common example is a JDBC driver when application source code compiles against the standard JDBC API and the driver implementation is loaded at runtime through configuration or service-provider discovery. Another example can be a logging implementation when source code compiles against a logging API but the concrete implementation is selected for execution. Runtime scope helps separate an API or compile-time contract from the implementation that must be present when the application starts. It is important to verify that the runtime artifact actually includes the required library. A project can compile successfully even when a runtime dependency is missing, so compile success alone is not sufficient validation. Run integration tests that exercise the relevant execution path and inspect the packaged artifact or deployment classpath. When diagnosing ClassNotFoundException or driver-loading failures, check whether the missing library was incorrectly declared with a scope that excludes it from runtime."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-6-section-5",
        "title": "Dependency Scopes — Test Scope and Isolated Test Libraries",
        "content": "Test scope is used for dependencies required only while compiling and executing tests. Maven makes test-scoped dependencies available on the test compile and test runtime classpaths, but they are not included in the main application runtime classpath and are not transitive to consumers as ordinary application dependencies. Typical examples include JUnit, Mockito, and test-specific assertion libraries. These tools support test code but should not normally become runtime requirements of the production application. Declaring them with test scope keeps the production dependency graph cleaner and avoids packaging unnecessary test frameworks into deployed artifacts. Maven's test lifecycle phases use the test classpath, so the test framework and its supporting libraries must be resolvable before tests can execute. A common mistake is to declare a library as test scope even though production source code imports its classes. That may cause compilation failures because the main compile classpath does not contain test-scoped dependencies. Another mistake is to use compile scope for every testing library, which unnecessarily expands the application dependency set. Keep test-only tools scoped correctly and verify both mvn test and the production packaging process."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-6-section-6",
        "title": "Dependency Scopes — System Scope, systemPath, and Maintenance Risks",
        "content": "System scope is a special scope for a dependency that is supplied from a specific file path on the local machine or build environment rather than being resolved from a Maven repository in the normal way. A system-scoped dependency requires the systemPath element, which points to the artifact file. This can make a build dependent on a particular directory layout, local installation, or agent configuration. Another developer or CI worker may not have the same file at that path, causing the build to fail even when the source repository is available. System dependencies also bypass normal repository-based artifact resolution and are not transitive in the usual way. For these reasons, system scope is strongly discouraged for modern project builds and is considered a legacy approach. A better solution is to publish the required library to an approved Maven repository, install a locally developed artifact into the local repository for development, or use a controlled repository manager for shared dependencies. If an old project still uses systemPath, document the required file, verify its provenance, and plan a migration so that builds become portable and reproducible across developer machines and CI agents."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-6-section-7",
        "title": "Dependency Scopes — Import Scope and BOM Version Alignment",
        "content": "Import scope is not an ordinary classpath scope for application libraries. It is used inside dependencyManagement when importing a dependency whose type is pom, typically a Bill of Materials. A BOM provides centrally managed dependency versions so that related libraries can be kept on a compatible version set. For example, a project can import a framework BOM and then declare the framework modules it actually uses without repeating every managed version. Maven incorporates the imported dependency management information into the effective project model. The imported BOM does not automatically add all of its listed libraries to the application's classpath. The project must still declare the individual dependencies it needs under dependencies. This distinction prevents a common misunderstanding: import does not mean that the BOM itself becomes a runtime JAR or that all managed artifacts are automatically packaged. Use import only in the correct dependencyManagement structure with type pom. When multiple BOMs manage overlapping coordinates, inspect the effective POM and understand precedence rather than assuming the desired version will be selected. BOM imports are useful for platform consistency, but they still require compatibility and upgrade review."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-6-section-8",
        "title": "Dependency Scopes — Transitivity and Scope Combination",
        "content": "Dependency scope affects how dependencies are exposed through the dependency graph. A compile dependency is generally transitive, while provided, test, and system dependencies are not propagated to downstream consumers as ordinary transitive dependencies. Runtime dependencies can be transitive and are made available for execution rather than compilation. When dependencies are reached through multiple paths, Maven combines scope information according to its dependency mediation rules to determine the effective scope in the consuming project. This means the scope visible in a dependency tree may differ from the scope written on one individual dependency declaration. Developers should not guess the final classpath from a single POM entry. Use mvn dependency:tree to inspect the actual graph and identify which path introduces each artifact. If a library unexpectedly appears at runtime, trace its incoming paths and scope relationships. If a required artifact is missing, determine whether an upstream dependency used a non-transitive scope or whether an exclusion removed it. Dependency scope decisions in reusable libraries are especially important because they influence what downstream applications receive. Validate changes by building both the library and a representative consumer project."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-6-section-9",
        "title": "Dependency Scopes — Production Classpath Validation and Troubleshooting Lab",
        "content": "Consider a Java web application that compiles successfully but fails after deployment with ClassNotFoundException for a library used by application code. First identify whether the missing class belongs to an application dependency, a platform-provided API, or a test-only library. Run mvn dependency:tree and inspect the dependency's declared and resolved scopes. Run mvn help:effective-pom to identify inherited scope configuration and managed dependency declarations. If the library is required by application code at runtime, confirm that it is not incorrectly marked as test or provided when the deployment environment does not supply it. If it is a container-provided API, verify that the target container really provides a compatible implementation before using provided. If the dependency is needed only by tests, keep it test-scoped and remove accidental production imports. Build the artifact again, inspect its packaged libraries, execute integration tests, and deploy it to a staging environment that resembles production. In CI, use clean builds and consistent JDK and Maven versions, and preserve dependency-tree output when investigating failures. Document the correct scope and the reason for it so future dependency changes do not reintroduce the same classpath problem."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "Transitive Dependencies",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-7-section-1",
        "title": "Transitive Dependencies — Dependency Graph Fundamentals",
        "content": "A transitive dependency is a library that a Maven project receives indirectly through another dependency rather than declaring directly in its own POM. A Java application often depends on a framework, and that framework may depend on several supporting libraries. Those supporting libraries can introduce further dependencies, creating a graph of related artifacts. Maven resolves this graph using artifact coordinates, dependency scopes, repository metadata, and dependency mediation rules. For example, an application may directly declare a web framework module, while that module brings logging APIs, utility libraries, and other components transitively. The application can use classes from these libraries even though it did not list every one of them explicitly, provided the resolved dependency graph exposes them on the relevant classpath. This behavior reduces the need to manually declare every low-level library, but it also means a small POM change can alter many resolved artifacts. Developers should understand that a dependency tree is a graph, not simply a flat list. Each edge represents a dependency relationship, and the same artifact may be reachable through multiple paths. Use mvn dependency:tree to inspect the resolved graph before making assumptions about what the build includes."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-7-section-2",
        "title": "Transitive Dependencies — Direct Dependencies Versus Indirect Dependencies",
        "content": "A direct dependency is explicitly declared by the project in its dependencies section. A transitive dependency is introduced through another dependency's own POM metadata. This distinction matters for ownership, upgrades, and compatibility. If application source code directly imports classes from a library, the application should normally declare that library directly instead of relying on an unrelated framework to bring it into the graph. Relying on an accidental transitive path can make the application fragile: an upstream framework may remove or change that dependency in a later release, causing the application to stop compiling. Direct declarations communicate the application's actual requirements and make version and scope decisions easier to review. Transitive dependencies are still useful because they allow library authors to describe the supporting components required by their artifacts. Maven resolves those requirements automatically. When investigating a dependency, identify whether it is direct or indirect in mvn dependency:tree output. If an application uses a transitive library directly, evaluate whether to declare it explicitly and manage its version through the project's dependency management policy. Do not add every transitive artifact as a direct dependency without reason; declare only genuine project requirements."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-7-section-3",
        "title": "Transitive Dependencies — Reading and Tracing the Maven Dependency Tree",
        "content": "The Maven dependency tree shows how artifacts enter a project and how their dependency paths are connected. Run mvn dependency:tree from the project directory to display the resolved dependency hierarchy. Each nested entry represents a dependency introduced by the artifact above it. The output can reveal which framework or library introduced an unexpected logging implementation, database driver, JSON library, or vulnerable component. Use mvn dependency:tree -Dverbose when supported by the installed dependency plugin to obtain additional information about omitted nodes and conflict resolution. You can also filter the output for a particular groupId or artifactId using plugin-supported options, which is useful in large applications. When tracing an issue, start with the exact artifact and follow its parent path back to the direct dependency that introduced it. Compare the tree before and after a POM change to identify newly added or upgraded transitive components. Save dependency-tree output in CI build logs when dependency changes are significant. Remember that the tree represents Maven's resolved model for the active project configuration; profiles, properties, Maven settings, and repository metadata can affect the result."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-7-section-4",
        "title": "Transitive Dependencies — Version Conflicts and Dependency Mediation",
        "content": "Version conflicts occur when different dependency paths request different versions of the same artifact coordinates. Maven applies dependency mediation to select one version for a given artifact in the resolved dependency graph. A key rule is nearest definition: the version reached through the shortest dependency path is generally selected. When competing versions are at the same depth, declaration order can influence selection. A direct dependency can be used to explicitly control the version when the selected transitive version is unsuitable. However, forcing a version is not automatically safe. A library may rely on methods or behavior available only in a particular release, so selecting another version can create binary incompatibility or runtime failures such as NoSuchMethodError, NoClassDefFoundError, or ClassCastException. Diagnose conflicts by examining mvn dependency:tree output and identifying all paths that request the artifact. Then review release notes and compatibility requirements before changing the version. Dependency convergence rules or suitable Maven Enforcer configuration can help detect multiple requested versions during builds. Validate any resolution with compilation, unit tests, integration tests, and runtime testing rather than relying only on successful dependency resolution."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-7-section-5",
        "title": "Transitive Dependencies — Excluding Unwanted Transitive Artifacts",
        "content": "Maven exclusions allow a project to prevent a particular transitive artifact from being included through a specific dependency path. An exclusion is declared inside the dependency whose transitive graph introduces the unwanted artifact, using the excluded artifact's groupId and artifactId. This is useful when an upstream library brings an incompatible logging implementation, an unwanted provider, or a component that the application replaces with an approved alternative. Exclusions are path-specific: excluding an artifact from one dependency does not necessarily remove it if another dependency path introduces the same artifact. Therefore, inspect the complete dependency tree after applying an exclusion. Avoid broad or unnecessary exclusions because the upstream library may genuinely require the removed component at runtime. Removing it can lead to missing classes or failures that appear only when a particular feature is executed. A safe process is to identify the introducing path, understand why the artifact is unwanted, add the narrow exclusion, inspect all remaining paths, and run tests that exercise the affected functionality. Document the reason for the exclusion so future maintainers know whether it is still necessary after upstream dependencies are upgraded."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-7-section-6",
        "title": "Transitive Dependencies — Optional Dependencies and Consumer Behavior",
        "content": "A dependency marked optional by its declaring library is not automatically propagated to downstream consumers as an ordinary transitive dependency. This is useful when a library supports integrations that only some applications need. For example, a reusable library may support several database engines or optional reporting integrations, but a consuming application should not be forced to include every integration. The library author can declare an optional dependency and document how consumers enable that capability. A consumer that needs the integration can declare the required artifact directly. Optional does not mean that the dependency is unnecessary inside the library's own build; it can still be needed to compile or test the library itself. It controls how the dependency is exposed to downstream projects. Developers should not confuse optional dependencies with exclusions. Optionality is declared by the dependency provider to avoid automatically imposing an integration on consumers, whereas an exclusion is configured by a consumer to remove an artifact from a particular dependency path. Test both the library and a representative consuming application to verify that optional features work when explicitly enabled and that applications without those features remain free of unnecessary dependencies."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-7-section-7",
        "title": "Transitive Dependencies — Scope Propagation and Classpath Effects",
        "content": "Transitive dependencies do not all appear on every classpath in the same way. Maven combines dependency scope information as it traverses the graph, and the resulting scope affects whether an artifact is available during compilation, testing, and execution. A compile-scoped dependency can expose its own transitive compile dependencies to a consumer. Runtime dependencies are available for execution but are not placed on the main compile classpath. Test and provided dependencies have more restricted propagation behavior. The effective scope of an artifact reached through multiple paths depends on Maven's scope combination and mediation rules, so reading one POM entry is not enough to predict the final classpath. A library may compile in its own module but fail in a consumer if an expected artifact is not exposed transitively. Conversely, an overly broad scope can make unwanted libraries available to downstream applications. Inspect the resolved dependency tree and test the consumer's actual build. For reusable libraries, define dependency scopes carefully and document any runtime requirements that consumers must provide themselves. This prevents accidental coupling between modules and reduces surprises when dependencies are upgraded."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-7-section-8",
        "title": "Transitive Dependencies — Cycles, Graph Size, and Dependency Governance",
        "content": "A well-designed dependency graph should be understandable, maintainable, and free from unnecessary coupling. Circular module dependencies can indicate an architectural problem: module A requires module B while module B also requires module A. Maven reactor builds and project model validation may expose cyclic relationships between modules, and such cycles should normally be resolved by redesigning module boundaries or extracting shared functionality into a separate module. Large transitive graphs create a different challenge. Every additional artifact can increase build time, introduce compatibility constraints, expand the runtime classpath, and add supply-chain exposure. Teams should periodically review dependency trees, remove unused direct dependencies, and avoid adding libraries for functionality already provided by approved platform components. Centralized dependency management, BOM imports, repository controls, vulnerability scanning, license review, and dependency convergence checks help maintain consistency. These controls should be integrated into CI rather than performed only after a production incident. Dependency governance should balance security and stability: upgrades need compatibility testing, while obsolete or vulnerable artifacts need a documented remediation plan. Keep the graph as small as practical without removing libraries that the application genuinely requires."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-7-section-9",
        "title": "Transitive Dependencies — Production Troubleshooting and Dependency Audit Lab",
        "content": "Consider a production service that begins failing after a framework upgrade. The application reports NoSuchMethodError in a library that was not explicitly declared in its POM. First reproduce the build with the same commit, JDK, Maven version, settings, and active profiles used by CI. Run mvn dependency:tree and trace the failing artifact to the direct dependency that introduced it. Compare the dependency tree with the previous successful build and identify changed versions or newly introduced paths. Run mvn help:effective-pom to inspect inherited dependency management and imported BOMs. Determine whether the framework upgrade changed a transitive version, whether another library requested an incompatible version, or whether an exclusion altered the graph. If necessary, manage a compatible version centrally or add a direct dependency after verifying compatibility with all consumers. Do not blindly force the newest version. Run clean compilation, unit tests, integration tests, and application startup tests. Review the packaged artifact and perform a staging deployment that exercises the failing feature. Record the root cause, dependency path, selected resolution, test evidence, and any security implications. This investigation process makes indirect dependency failures traceable and prevents temporary fixes from creating hidden runtime problems."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "Build Lifecycle",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-8-section-1",
        "title": "Build Lifecycle — Maven Build Model and Lifecycle Fundamentals",
        "content": "The Maven build lifecycle is the organized sequence of stages through which Maven processes a project. It defines a standard execution model so developers do not need to manually decide every operation required to validate, compile, test, package, and distribute an application. A lifecycle is made up of ordered phases. When a user invokes a phase, Maven executes that phase and all earlier phases in the same lifecycle. The actual work is performed by plugin goals bound to lifecycle phases. For example, compiling Java source code is commonly performed by a compiler plugin goal bound to the compile phase, while packaging is performed by a packaging-related goal bound to package. This separation is important: a lifecycle phase represents a standard point in the build process, while a plugin goal performs a concrete operation. Maven defines three built-in lifecycles named default, clean, and site. A project can use the standard lifecycle without manually configuring every plugin because packaging types provide default lifecycle bindings. Understanding this model helps teams create repeatable local and CI builds, select the correct command for a task, and diagnose why a command executes more operations than expected."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-8-section-2",
        "title": "Build Lifecycle — Default Lifecycle and Application Build Progression",
        "content": "The default lifecycle handles the main process of building and deploying a project artifact. Its phases cover validation, source generation, resource processing, compilation, testing, packaging, verification, installation, and deployment. Common phases include validate, compile, test, package, verify, install, and deploy. These phases are ordered, so invoking a later phase also executes the preceding phases in that lifecycle. For example, mvn package normally runs the required earlier default-lifecycle phases before creating the package. It does not automatically execute phases that come after package, such as install or deploy. The install phase places the built artifact in the local Maven repository so other local projects can use it. The deploy phase publishes the artifact to a configured remote repository when the project and repository configuration allow it. The default lifecycle is the primary lifecycle used for ordinary Java application builds. Teams should select the phase that matches the intended outcome: test for test execution, package for artifact creation, verify for additional verification, install for local repository installation, and deploy for remote publication. Avoid invoking deploy casually from a developer machine because it can publish artifacts to shared repositories."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-8-section-3",
        "title": "Build Lifecycle — Clean Lifecycle and Workspace Reset",
        "content": "The clean lifecycle is responsible for removing files generated by earlier builds. Its commonly used phases are pre-clean, clean, and post-clean. The clean phase is normally bound to a clean plugin goal that removes the project's build output directory, usually target. Running mvn clean helps eliminate stale compiled classes, generated resources, and previous package files that could otherwise affect a build. It is especially useful when investigating problems caused by outdated generated output or when validating that the project can be built from source without relying on leftover files. The clean lifecycle is separate from the default lifecycle. Therefore, mvn package does not automatically run clean, and mvn clean does not compile or package the application. Developers commonly combine them as mvn clean package to remove previous output and then execute the default lifecycle through package. In multi-module builds, clean is applied to the relevant project modules according to the reactor build. Be aware that custom build plugins or generated files outside the standard output directory may require additional cleanup configuration. Clean should remove build output, not source files, configuration, or user data."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-8-section-4",
        "title": "Build Lifecycle — Site Lifecycle and Project Documentation",
        "content": "The site lifecycle is intended for generating and publishing project documentation. Its phases include pre-site, site, post-site, and site-deploy. The site phase can execute configured documentation and reporting goals to create a project site containing information such as project reports, test results, dependency information, and other configured documentation. The exact output depends on the Maven Site Plugin version and the reports and plugins configured by the project. The site-deploy phase can publish the generated site to a configured destination when the required deployment configuration is available. This lifecycle is separate from the default application packaging lifecycle: generating a project site does not mean that the application JAR or WAR has been built and deployed. Teams can use the site lifecycle to make technical documentation and build reports easier to access. In enterprise environments, site generation may be part of a documentation pipeline rather than every application build. Review plugin versions, report configuration, and publishing credentials before enabling site deployment in CI. Keep documentation publication permissions separate from ordinary source compilation and test permissions."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-8-section-5",
        "title": "Build Lifecycle — Lifecycle Phases, Ordering, and Command Behavior",
        "content": "A Maven lifecycle phase is a named stage in the build process. Phases have a defined order, and invoking a phase causes Maven to execute earlier phases in the same lifecycle before reaching the requested phase. For example, invoking mvn test executes the earlier default-lifecycle phases needed to prepare and compile the project before running tests. Invoking mvn package also runs the phases that precede package, including the test phase in the normal default lifecycle sequence. A frequent misunderstanding is assuming that a phase executes only one isolated action. In reality, the requested phase represents a point in an ordered lifecycle. Maven can also execute goals directly, such as mvn compiler:compile, but direct goal invocation does not provide the same complete lifecycle progression as invoking a lifecycle phase. Multiple lifecycle phases can be requested in one command, and Maven processes them in the supplied order while applying lifecycle rules. The clean and default lifecycles remain distinct, so mvn clean package explicitly requests work from both. Understanding phase ordering helps explain why builds run tests, process resources, or create artifacts even when the command appears to request only one final phase."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-8-section-6",
        "title": "Build Lifecycle — Packaging Bindings and Automatic Plugin Execution",
        "content": "Maven packaging types influence which plugin goals are automatically bound to lifecycle phases. Common packaging types include jar, war, pom, and other supported formats. For a JAR project, Maven provides default bindings for operations such as resource processing, Java compilation, test compilation, test execution, and JAR creation. A WAR project has packaging-specific bindings that support web application packaging. A POM project is commonly used for parent and aggregator projects and does not produce an ordinary application JAR. These default bindings explain why a simple command such as mvn package can perform several build operations even when the POM does not explicitly list every plugin execution. The effective behavior depends on the packaging type, Maven version, lifecycle mapping, and any custom plugin configuration. When a project produces an unexpected artifact or skips an expected operation, inspect the packaging value and effective POM. Do not assume every packaging type has identical lifecycle bindings. Teams should configure additional plugin executions only when the required behavior is not already provided by the packaging defaults, avoiding duplicate executions and unnecessary build complexity."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-8-section-7",
        "title": "Build Lifecycle — Custom Lifecycle Bindings and Execution Configuration",
        "content": "Projects can extend the standard Maven build by configuring plugin executions and binding plugin goals to lifecycle phases. A plugin execution can specify an execution ID, one or more goals, a phase, and configuration values. For example, a project may configure a code-generation goal to run during generate-sources so generated Java files are available before compilation. Another project may bind a verification goal to the verify phase so additional quality checks run before an artifact is accepted. Maven merges plugin configuration from parent POMs and child modules according to model inheritance rules, so the effective configuration may include values not visible in the current module's POM. Use stable execution IDs and document why a custom binding is needed. Avoid binding expensive or environment-specific tasks to phases that every developer and CI build must execute unless that behavior is intentional. When a goal appears to execute twice, inspect plugin executions inherited from parent POMs and packaging defaults. Use mvn help:effective-pom and Maven debug output to investigate the final configuration. Keep lifecycle customization predictable so developers can understand what a standard command will execute."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-8-section-8",
        "title": "Build Lifecycle — Multi-Module Reactor and CI Pipeline Execution",
        "content": "In a multi-module Maven project, the reactor coordinates the build of multiple related modules. Maven reads the aggregator POM, identifies the modules, constructs their relationships, and builds them in an order that respects inter-module dependencies. Invoking a lifecycle phase from the root project can apply that phase to the selected modules. Options such as -pl can select projects, while -am can also build required projects in the reactor. These options are useful in CI pipelines where a change affects only a subset of a large repository, although the dependency relationships must be understood before skipping modules. A typical CI pipeline may execute mvn clean verify to clean previous output, compile the project, run tests, package artifacts, and perform verification checks. Artifact publication should be a deliberate later step, often using a controlled deploy job with appropriate credentials. Use consistent Maven and JDK versions across build agents, preserve build logs, and make test failures visible as pipeline failures. In multi-module environments, confirm whether the command was executed from the aggregator root or an individual module because the selected reactor and lifecycle execution scope can differ."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-8-section-9",
        "title": "Build Lifecycle — Execution Troubleshooting and Production Build Lab",
        "content": "When a Maven build executes an unexpected goal, skips a phase, or fails at a particular lifecycle stage, first identify the exact command, working directory, Maven version, JDK version, active profiles, and module selection. Run the command again with normal logging and use mvn -X when detailed debug output is necessary. Inspect the phase where the failure occurs and determine whether the problem is in resource processing, compilation, test execution, packaging, verification, installation, or deployment. Check the effective POM to identify inherited plugin versions and executions. If stale generated output is suspected, run mvn clean followed by the required lifecycle phase. If a plugin goal is not executing, verify its phase binding, plugin configuration, packaging type, and whether the execution is inherited or disabled. In a multi-module build, inspect reactor summaries and confirm the intended modules were selected. For a production CI pipeline, a controlled sequence such as mvn clean verify followed by a separately authorized artifact publication step provides a clear boundary between validation and distribution. Preserve logs and the generated artifact, reproduce the failure in a consistent environment, and document the root cause and corrective action."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "Phases and Goals",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-9-section-1",
        "title": "Phases and Goals — Understanding Maven Execution Units",
        "content": "Maven separates build execution into lifecycle phases and plugin goals. A phase represents a meaningful stage in a standard build process, such as validating the project, compiling source code, running tests, or packaging the application. A goal represents a specific task implemented by a Maven plugin, such as compiling Java files, creating a JAR, generating documentation, or executing tests. Maven connects these concepts through lifecycle bindings. When a user requests a lifecycle phase, Maven executes the goals bound to that phase and the required preceding phases in lifecycle order. A plugin goal can also be invoked directly using plugin-prefix or fully qualified plugin coordinates. Understanding the distinction prevents a common misunderstanding: a phase is not itself a plugin goal, and a goal is not necessarily a complete lifecycle. For example, mvn package requests a lifecycle phase, whereas mvn compiler:compile invokes a specific plugin goal. The lifecycle provides an ordered build model, while plugins provide the actual implementation of build operations. This design allows projects to use consistent commands while selecting and configuring the plugins that perform the work."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-9-section-2",
        "title": "Phases and Goals — Standard Maven Lifecycle Phase Sequence",
        "content": "The default Maven lifecycle contains an ordered sequence of phases used to build and deliver an application. Important phases include validate, initialize, generate-sources, process-sources, generate-resources, process-resources, compile, process-classes, generate-test-sources, process-test-sources, generate-test-resources, process-test-resources, test-compile, process-test-classes, test, prepare-package, package, pre-integration-test, integration-test, post-integration-test, verify, install, and deploy. A requested phase causes Maven to execute that phase and all earlier phases in the same lifecycle. For example, mvn test executes the required preceding default-lifecycle phases before reaching test. mvn package progresses further and reaches artifact packaging. mvn verify continues through verification-related phases, while mvn install installs the built artifact into the local Maven repository. The deploy phase publishes the artifact to a configured remote repository. Not every project uses every phase, and plugin bindings determine which concrete goals execute at particular points. Teams should understand the phase sequence before designing CI pipelines so they do not run unnecessary commands or accidentally skip required validation steps."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-9-section-3",
        "title": "Phases and Goals — Lifecycle Phase Execution and Command Progression",
        "content": "A Maven lifecycle command is cumulative. Running mvn compile does not execute only the compile phase; Maven executes all preceding phases in the default lifecycle that apply to the project before reaching compile. Similarly, mvn test progresses through validation, resource processing, compilation, test resource processing, test compilation, and test execution according to the configured lifecycle bindings. Running mvn package continues beyond tests and creates the configured artifact. This behavior makes lifecycle commands predictable and reduces the need to manually invoke every build operation. A common mistake is to assume that mvn package automatically performs every possible verification or production deployment task. It only executes the phases up to package in the default lifecycle. For stronger validation, teams often use mvn verify when integration checks and verification goals are bound appropriately. The exact work performed also depends on packaging type, plugin configuration, active profiles, and custom executions. Inspect Maven output to understand which goals were actually executed. When troubleshooting skipped tests or missing packaging steps, check the requested phase and the plugin goals bound to it rather than assuming the lifecycle command performed every desired operation."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-9-section-4",
        "title": "Phases and Goals — Plugin Goals and Direct Goal Invocation",
        "content": "A Maven plugin provides one or more goals that perform specific build tasks. Goals are invoked through a plugin prefix when Maven can resolve that prefix, or through a fully qualified form containing the plugin groupId, artifactId, version, and goal. For example, mvn compiler:compile invokes the compile goal of the Maven Compiler Plugin, while mvn surefire:test invokes the test goal of the Maven Surefire Plugin. A command such as mvn help:effective-pom invokes a goal that displays the effective project model. Direct goal invocation is useful for focused operations, diagnostics, and tasks that are not conveniently reached through a lifecycle phase. However, invoking a goal directly does not automatically execute all preceding lifecycle phases. Running a packaging-related goal alone may therefore fail if required compilation or resource processing has not already happened. For normal project builds, prefer lifecycle commands such as mvn package or mvn verify. Use direct goal invocation when the task is intentionally independent or when investigating a specific plugin behavior. Pin plugin versions in project configuration where reproducibility is important."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-9-section-5",
        "title": "Phases and Goals — Lifecycle Bindings by Packaging Type",
        "content": "Maven packaging determines important default plugin-goal bindings in the default lifecycle. A JAR project, WAR project, and POM project do not necessarily execute identical goals when the same lifecycle phase is requested. For a typical JAR project, compilation and JAR creation are associated with the relevant lifecycle phases. A WAR project uses packaging-specific behavior to assemble web application content and produce a WAR artifact. A POM project is commonly used for parent or aggregator configuration and does not create an ordinary application JAR. Maven's default lifecycle mappings connect packaging types to plugin goals so developers can use familiar lifecycle commands without manually specifying every operation. These bindings can also be extended or customized through plugin configuration. When a project behaves differently after changing packaging, inspect the effective POM and Maven build output to identify which goals are bound. Do not assume that all packaging types execute the same plugin goals. CI pipelines should use commands appropriate to the artifact being produced and verify the actual output file and its contents."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-9-section-6",
        "title": "Phases and Goals — Plugin Executions and Custom Phase Bindings",
        "content": "A plugin execution connects one or more plugin goals to a lifecycle phase through the plugin configuration in pom.xml. An execution can have an identifier, a phase, a list of goals, and configuration specific to that execution. This allows a project to run code generation, static analysis, packaging preparation, or other custom operations at the correct point in its build. For example, a code-generation plugin can execute before compilation so generated Java files are available to the compiler. A plugin can also define executions for different phases or configure multiple executions with distinct settings. Maven combines plugin configuration inherited from parent POMs and active profiles when constructing the effective model. Poorly designed executions can cause duplicate work, unexpected ordering, or environment-specific build behavior. Keep execution IDs meaningful and configure the phase explicitly when the intended binding is not obvious. Inspect mvn help:effective-pom and the build log when a custom goal does not execute as expected. Ensure that generated outputs are placed in appropriate build directories and that repeated clean builds produce consistent results."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-9-section-7",
        "title": "Phases and Goals — Clean Lifecycle and Site Lifecycle Commands",
        "content": "Maven provides three standard lifecycles: default, clean, and site. The default lifecycle handles the main project build, including compilation, testing, packaging, verification, installation, and deployment. The clean lifecycle removes files generated by earlier builds. Its important phases include pre-clean, clean, and post-clean. Running mvn clean invokes the clean phase and its preceding phase, allowing the configured clean plugin to remove the build output directory. The site lifecycle generates project documentation and reports, with phases such as pre-site, site, post-site, and site-deploy. These lifecycles are separate from the default lifecycle. A command such as mvn clean package invokes clean and then the default lifecycle through package. This is useful in CI because it avoids relying on stale generated files from an earlier run. However, clean does not mean that every external cache or the local Maven repository is erased. The site lifecycle is useful when a project maintains generated documentation, but it is not required for every application build. Choose lifecycle commands based on the actual pipeline requirement."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-9-section-8",
        "title": "Phases and Goals — Command-Line Options and Build Control",
        "content": "Maven command-line options control how phases and goals are executed. The -D option defines a system or Maven user property, such as -DskipTests, which is commonly used to configure plugin behavior. The -P option activates one or more Maven profiles, while -pl selects projects in a multi-module reactor and -am can also build required projects in that reactor. The -T option controls parallel reactor execution where supported and appropriate. The -o option requests offline mode, which can be useful when all required artifacts are already available locally. The -U option forces checks for updated snapshots and certain metadata during dependency resolution. The -X option enables detailed debug output for troubleshooting, while -q reduces normal output. Options must be understood carefully: -DskipTests commonly skips test execution but still compiles tests, whereas -Dmaven.test.skip=true can skip test compilation and execution through plugin configuration. These options can change the quality and reproducibility of a build. Production CI should avoid silently skipping required tests and should record important command-line properties and profile selections."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-9-section-9",
        "title": "Phases and Goals — Multi-Module Reactor and Execution Ordering",
        "content": "A multi-module Maven project can contain a parent or aggregator POM and several related modules. Maven's reactor collects the projects, determines their relationships, and orders build execution so required upstream modules are available before dependent modules are processed. A command such as mvn verify from the aggregator directory can build the participating modules through the requested phase. The -pl option can select particular modules, and -am can include required projects in the reactor. Plugin goals and lifecycle phases execute within each module according to the reactor build process and project configuration. Parallel execution can improve performance, but it must be used only when modules and plugins are safe for the selected concurrency model. Shared output directories, non-thread-safe plugins, and hidden cross-module assumptions can create failures. When a module succeeds locally but fails in the full reactor, inspect module ordering, inter-module dependencies, profiles, and plugin configuration. CI pipelines should build the intended reactor scope and retain logs that identify the module and goal responsible for a failure. Validate both selected-module builds and full project builds when changes affect shared modules."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-9-section-10",
        "title": "Phases and Goals — CI/CD Build Design and Execution Troubleshooting Lab",
        "content": "A production CI pipeline should select Maven lifecycle phases according to the validation and delivery guarantees it must provide. A typical pull-request workflow may execute mvn clean verify so that compilation, unit tests, integration checks configured in the project, and verification goals can run before approval. A release pipeline may execute mvn deploy after the required checks and release configuration have passed, publishing the artifact to an approved repository. Direct plugin goals should be used only when the pipeline intentionally needs a focused task, such as generating a report or inspecting the effective POM. If a goal is not executing, check whether it is bound to the requested phase, whether the relevant profile is active, and whether the plugin version and execution configuration are correct. If tests are unexpectedly skipped, inspect command-line properties and Surefire or Failsafe configuration. If a build fails after a packaging change, compare the effective POM and lifecycle output. Keep JDK and Maven versions consistent across agents, avoid unnecessary test-skipping flags, and preserve build logs. Document the selected lifecycle command, active profiles, required environment variables, artifact location, and publication conditions so the pipeline remains reproducible and auditable."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "Maven Plugins",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-10-section-1",
        "title": "Maven Plugins — Architecture, Responsibilities, and Build Extension Model",
        "content": "Maven plugins provide the executable functionality behind many project build operations. Maven itself defines project structure, dependency resolution, lifecycle phases, and the mechanism for invoking plugins, while plugins implement concrete tasks such as compiling Java source code, copying resources, running tests, creating archives, generating reports, and publishing artifacts. A plugin contains one or more goals, and each goal performs a specific operation. Plugins can be invoked directly from the command line or bound to lifecycle phases through project configuration and packaging defaults. This architecture makes Maven extensible: a project can introduce additional build operations without changing Maven's core. Plugins are normally resolved as artifacts from configured repositories and can have their own dependencies and configuration parameters. The plugin version, goal, execution configuration, and active project properties determine the behavior of a build task. A reliable project should declare important plugin versions explicitly rather than depending entirely on environment-specific defaults. Understanding the plugin model helps developers investigate build logs, control build behavior, and create consistent CI pipelines. A plugin is not the same as a project dependency: plugins execute build-time tasks, while ordinary dependencies provide libraries used by application or test code."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-10-section-2",
        "title": "Maven Plugins — Plugin Coordinates, Prefixes, and Version Resolution",
        "content": "A Maven plugin is identified by artifact coordinates, including groupId, artifactId, and version. Many commonly used plugins are published under the org.apache.maven.plugins group, while other plugins use their own organization or vendor group IDs. Maven supports plugin prefixes that make commands shorter. For example, a command such as mvn compiler:compile uses the compiler prefix to locate the Maven Compiler Plugin and invoke its compile goal. Prefix resolution depends on plugin metadata and repository configuration. A fully qualified plugin goal can be invoked using its complete plugin coordinates and goal name, which is useful when prefix resolution is ambiguous or a specific plugin version must be selected. Plugin versions should be controlled in the POM, commonly through the build plugins section or inherited parent configuration. Relying on an implicit version can lead to different behavior across Maven installations or over time as metadata changes. When investigating plugin resolution failures, check the groupId, artifactId, version, configured plugin repositories, network access, and Maven settings. Pinning versions and using controlled repositories makes plugin execution more predictable across local development machines and CI agents."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-10-section-3",
        "title": "Maven Plugins — Goals, Parameters, and Goal-Specific Behavior",
        "content": "A plugin goal is an individual operation provided by a Maven plugin. One plugin can expose multiple goals, each with a separate purpose and configuration. For example, the Maven Compiler Plugin provides goals for compiling main source code and test source code. The Maven Clean Plugin provides operations that remove generated build files, while the Maven Resources Plugin handles project resource processing. Goals may require parameters such as source directories, target directories, encoding, output locations, or execution flags. Some parameters have defaults, and others can be supplied through plugin configuration or user properties. A goal may also have prerequisites or require certain project information to exist before it can run. Directly invoking a goal does not necessarily execute earlier lifecycle phases, so a goal that expects compiled classes or generated resources may fail when those prerequisites have not been produced. Read the plugin documentation for the exact goal, parameter names, defaults, supported user properties, and version compatibility. Inspect Maven debug output when a configured parameter appears to be ignored. Correct goal selection and parameter configuration are essential for predictable build behavior."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-10-section-4",
        "title": "Maven Plugins — Declaring Plugins in the POM and Configuration Structure",
        "content": "Plugins used by a project are commonly declared inside the build/plugins element of pom.xml. A plugin declaration can specify its groupId, artifactId, version, configuration, and one or more executions. The configuration element provides plugin-specific settings, while executions connect selected goals to lifecycle phases and can define execution-specific configuration. A project may also declare plugins under pluginManagement in a parent POM. PluginManagement supplies managed plugin configuration and versions to child projects, but it does not by itself cause every managed plugin to execute. A child project generally needs a corresponding plugin declaration under build/plugins to activate that managed plugin configuration. This distinction is similar to the difference between dependencyManagement and dependencies. Keep configuration close to the plugin responsible for the task and use meaningful execution IDs. Avoid duplicating the same configuration across many modules when it can be maintained centrally in a parent POM. After changing plugin configuration, inspect the effective POM and build logs to confirm the intended settings are applied. Correct POM structure improves maintainability and reduces differences between modules."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-10-section-5",
        "title": "Maven Plugins — Lifecycle Binding and Execution Configuration",
        "content": "Maven plugins can execute automatically when a lifecycle phase is requested because goals are bound to phases through default packaging mappings or explicit plugin executions. An execution can define an ID, a phase, a list of goals, and configuration for that execution. For example, a code-generation goal may be bound to generate-sources so that generated Java files are available before compilation. A static analysis goal may be bound to verify so that its checks run later in the build. A plugin can have multiple executions, each with different phases and settings. Maven combines applicable plugin configuration from parent POMs, active profiles, and the current project when constructing the effective model. If the same goal is configured in more than one place, the resulting behavior may be difficult to understand without inspecting that model. Avoid binding expensive operations to early phases unless they are genuinely required at that point. Use execution IDs that describe the task and keep phase selection intentional. When a goal does not run, check whether the plugin is declared, the execution is active, the requested phase reaches the configured phase, and the relevant profile is enabled."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-10-section-6",
        "title": "Maven Plugins — Essential Build Plugins and Their Practical Roles",
        "content": "Maven projects commonly use several plugins to perform standard build operations. The Maven Compiler Plugin compiles main and test Java source code and supports configuration such as the Java release level. The Maven Resources Plugin copies and filters project resources. The Maven Surefire Plugin executes unit tests during the test phase, while the Maven Failsafe Plugin is commonly used for integration tests through its integration-test and verify goals. The Maven JAR Plugin creates JAR artifacts, and the Maven WAR Plugin packages web applications. The Maven Clean Plugin removes generated build output. The Maven Install Plugin places an artifact in the local Maven repository, while the Maven Deploy Plugin publishes it to a configured remote repository. The Maven Dependency Plugin supports dependency inspection and related operations, and the Maven Help Plugin provides diagnostic goals such as effective POM inspection. These plugins serve different purposes and should be configured according to project requirements. Do not add every available plugin automatically. Select only the plugins needed by the build, pin appropriate versions, and validate their behavior against the project's JDK, Maven version, packaging type, and release process."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-10-section-7",
        "title": "Maven Plugins — Plugin Version Management and Reproducible Builds",
        "content": "Plugin version management is an important part of build reproducibility. A project that does not specify plugin versions may resolve behavior differently as plugin metadata and Maven environments change. A newer plugin release can introduce changed defaults, new parameters, deprecated behavior, or different compatibility requirements. Teams should define versions for important build plugins and manage them centrally through a parent POM or pluginManagement when appropriate. Version upgrades should be reviewed using plugin release notes and compatibility information. Validate upgrades with clean builds, unit tests, integration tests, packaging checks, and the relevant CI pipeline. A controlled upgrade process should also consider the minimum Maven version and JDK version supported by the plugin. Avoid changing several unrelated plugin versions in one update because failures become harder to isolate. Store the approved plugin configuration in source control and keep developer and CI environments aligned. For long-lived enterprise projects, document the reason for important plugin versions and define ownership for maintenance. Reproducible builds require more than pinning dependencies: build plugins and their behavior must also be controlled."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-10-section-8",
        "title": "Maven Plugins — Plugin Repositories, Resolution, and Security",
        "content": "Maven resolves plugins from configured plugin repositories, which are distinct in project-model configuration from ordinary dependency repositories. A plugin repository is declared using pluginRepositories in the POM or supplied through applicable Maven settings and repository-management configuration. Organizations often route plugin and dependency downloads through an approved repository manager to improve availability, control external access, and support auditing. A plugin resolution failure may be caused by incorrect coordinates, unavailable versions, repository metadata, authentication, network restrictions, proxy configuration, or an unavailable repository. Plugin artifacts are executable build components, so they are part of the software supply-chain trust boundary. Use trusted repositories, review plugin ownership and maintenance status, and restrict uncontrolled plugin sources in enterprise environments. Avoid downloading plugin JAR files from arbitrary websites and placing them directly into build directories. CI credentials should have only the permissions required to resolve or publish artifacts. When a plugin cannot be downloaded, inspect Maven error output and settings configuration before changing the POM. Repository controls and plugin version governance reduce the risk of compromised or unexpected build behavior."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-10-section-9",
        "title": "Maven Plugins — Custom Plugin Development and Internal Build Automation",
        "content": "Maven supports custom plugins when an organization needs repeatable build operations that are not adequately provided by existing plugins. A custom plugin is developed as a Maven project and exposes goals implemented through plugin components, commonly called Mojos. A Mojo defines the operation, its parameters, and how it interacts with Maven project information and the build environment. Parameters can be supplied through plugin configuration, project properties, or supported defaults. Custom goals can be invoked directly or bound to lifecycle phases in consuming projects. For example, an organization may create a plugin that validates internal deployment metadata, checks naming conventions, or generates a standardized release manifest. Custom plugins should have clear parameter documentation, meaningful error messages, unit tests, and integration tests using realistic Maven projects. Avoid embedding environment-specific secrets or machine-specific paths in plugin logic. Publish internal plugins to an approved repository and version them like other software artifacts. Maintain compatibility with the Maven versions and JDK versions used by consuming projects. Custom plugin development is valuable when it centralizes repeated build policy, but it should not be used to recreate standard functionality already available through maintained plugins."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-10-section-10",
        "title": "Maven Plugins — CI/CD Integration, Diagnostics, and Production Build Lab",
        "content": "A production CI pipeline should execute Maven plugins in a controlled and observable way. A typical pipeline can run mvn clean verify to execute the required lifecycle and plugin goals through verification. Plugin versions should be declared in source control, and the pipeline should use a consistent Maven and JDK environment. When a plugin fails, identify the exact plugin coordinates, goal, execution ID, lifecycle phase, and configuration reported in the build log. Run mvn help:effective-pom to inspect inherited plugin settings and active profiles. Use mvn -X only when detailed debug information is needed, because debug output can be large and may reveal sensitive configuration if logs are not handled carefully. If a goal is missing, verify that its execution is bound to a phase reached by the requested command. If a plugin cannot be resolved, inspect pluginRepositories, Maven settings, proxy configuration, authentication, and repository availability. If an upgrade introduces a failure, compare plugin versions and configuration with the previous successful build. Validate the fix using clean builds, tests, artifact inspection, and staging execution. Preserve useful logs and document the plugin-related root cause so future pipeline failures can be diagnosed efficiently."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "Maven Profiles",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-11-section-1",
        "title": "Maven Profiles — Purpose, Build Variants, and Profile Model",
        "content": "Maven profiles allow a project to customize selected build settings for different environments, operating conditions, or development requirements without maintaining separate copies of the POM. A profile can define properties, dependencies, plugin configuration, repositories, resource settings, and other supported project model elements. For example, a team may need different configuration values for local development, testing, staging, and production builds. Profiles provide a way to activate those variations while keeping the main project structure consistent. A profile is not a separate Maven project or a replacement for the standard lifecycle. It modifies the effective project model when it is active. Profiles can be declared in a project's POM, a parent POM, or Maven settings.xml, subject to the different activation and portability rules of those locations. A profile should represent a meaningful build variation rather than become a collection of unrelated switches. Keep environment-specific values explicit and avoid placing credentials or secret values directly in version-controlled POM files. Understanding profile behavior is essential when the same project must build consistently across developer machines and automated CI/CD environments."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-11-section-2",
        "title": "Maven Profiles — POM Profile Structure and Supported Configuration",
        "content": "A profile inside pom.xml is declared under the profiles element and contains an ID that identifies it. Within a profile, developers can configure supported project model elements such as properties, dependencies, dependency management, repositories, plugin repositories, build configuration, and reporting settings. The exact supported structure follows Maven's POM model. For example, a profile can define a property named deployment.environment and configure a plugin to consume that property. A profile can also add a dependency needed only for a particular build variant, although dependency changes should be carefully reviewed because they can affect the resulting artifact. The profile's activation element controls when Maven selects it automatically. A profile may instead be activated explicitly using a command-line option. Profile IDs should be descriptive and stable because developers and pipelines refer to them by ID. Avoid defining the same property with conflicting values in multiple active profiles unless the precedence is intentional and understood. After changing a profile, inspect the effective POM to verify which configuration Maven actually applies. A well-structured profile makes the build variation understandable without hiding important behavior in scattered configuration files."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-11-section-3",
        "title": "Maven Profiles — Explicit Activation and Deactivation from the Command Line",
        "content": "A profile can be activated explicitly by using the Maven -P option followed by its profile ID. For example, mvn clean verify -Pstaging activates the profile named staging while executing the requested lifecycle. Multiple profiles can be selected in a command by supplying their IDs in the same profile selection argument, separated by commas. A profile can be explicitly deactivated by prefixing its ID with an exclamation mark or, depending on shell interpretation, by using the supported negation syntax with suitable quoting. This is useful when a profile is automatically activated by a property or environment condition but must be disabled for a particular build. Command-line profile selection is often preferable in CI pipelines because the intended build variant is visible in the pipeline definition. Do not assume that naming a profile in a command changes every application runtime setting; Maven profiles modify the build model, and the actual artifact or deployment configuration must consume the selected values. Document the expected profile IDs and validate the effective model for each supported build command. Explicit activation helps teams avoid depending on undocumented machine-specific conditions."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-11-section-4",
        "title": "Maven Profiles — Automatic Activation by JDK, OS, Property, and File",
        "content": "Maven supports automatic profile activation based on conditions defined in the profile's activation element. A profile can activate when a particular JDK version or version range is detected, when operating system attributes match, when a system property has a specified value, or when a file exists or is missing at a configured location. For example, a development-only profile might activate when a developer-specific property is supplied, while a profile could activate for a particular operating system when a platform-specific build tool is required. Property activation can check whether a property exists or whether it has a particular value. File activation can be useful for local build customization, but it can also make builds dependent on files that are not present in CI agents. Automatic activation should be used carefully because developers may not realize why a profile became active. Check Maven's profile activation output and effective POM when a build behaves differently across machines. Avoid using fragile filesystem paths or broad conditions that unintentionally activate profiles. For predictable production builds, prefer explicit profile selection where practical and keep automatic activation conditions narrow, documented, and testable."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-11-section-5",
        "title": "Maven Profiles — Settings Profiles, User Configuration, and Environment Separation",
        "content": "Maven profiles can also be declared in settings.xml, which is commonly located in a user's Maven configuration directory or supplied through an alternative settings file. Settings profiles are useful for user- or environment-specific configuration such as repository properties and repository definitions that should not be committed into a project's POM. They are particularly relevant when developers or CI agents use different repository managers, mirrors, or credentials. A settings profile can be activated through the activeProfiles section or through supported activation conditions. Settings profiles have a different purpose and a more limited configuration model than POM profiles; they should not be treated as a way to place arbitrary project build configuration outside the POM. Credentials belong in appropriately protected settings configuration or a secure credential mechanism, not in source-controlled project files. CI systems should inject or securely mount settings files with controlled access and avoid printing their contents into logs. When a build works locally but fails on an agent, compare the effective settings, active settings profiles, mirrors, server credentials, and repository configuration. Separating user-specific repository access from portable project build logic improves security and consistency."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-11-section-6",
        "title": "Maven Profiles — Profile Properties, Resource Filtering, and Build Configuration",
        "content": "Profiles commonly define properties that allow selected build configuration to change without duplicating the complete POM. A profile might define an environment identifier, a feature flag, or a non-secret endpoint value that is consumed by plugin configuration or resource filtering. Resource filtering allows Maven to replace supported placeholders in selected resource files with values available during the build. This can be useful for generating environment-specific configuration files, but teams must carefully control which files are filtered. Binary files and resources containing values that must remain unchanged should not be filtered. Avoid placing passwords, tokens, private keys, or other secrets in profile properties because the values may appear in effective POM output, build logs, or packaged artifacts. A safer approach is to inject secrets at deployment or runtime using a secure secret-management mechanism. When profiles change resource directories or plugin behavior, verify the resulting files inside the built artifact rather than assuming the configuration was applied. Keep the filtering configuration explicit and test each supported build variant. This makes it easier to detect missing placeholders, incorrect endpoints, and accidental inclusion of development-only settings in production packages."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-11-section-7",
        "title": "Maven Profiles — Inheritance, Parent POMs, and Multi-Module Projects",
        "content": "Profiles can be declared in parent POMs and inherited project models, but profile activation and inheritance behavior must be understood carefully. A parent POM can centralize common build configuration and provide profile definitions for related modules. However, a profile is not simply a universal switch that guarantees identical activation in every child module. Profile activation is evaluated in the context of the project and Maven environment, and settings-based profiles have their own activation rules. In a multi-module build, different modules may have different packaging types, dependencies, and plugin requirements even when they share a parent. Avoid assuming that activating one profile automatically makes every module produce the same output. Validate the effective POM for representative modules and inspect the reactor build logs. Keep shared properties and common plugin settings in suitable parent configuration, while keeping module-specific configuration close to the module that needs it. If several modules require the same environment variation, document the expected profile behavior and test the complete reactor command. This prevents inconsistent artifacts and unexpected differences between modules in a release pipeline."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-11-section-8",
        "title": "Maven Profiles — Profile Precedence, Conflicts, and Effective Model Inspection",
        "content": "A Maven build can have multiple profiles active at the same time. When profiles contribute overlapping properties or configuration, the resulting effective model depends on Maven's model-building and merging rules, profile location, inheritance, and activation context. Teams should avoid relying on accidental activation order to resolve conflicting values. Use clear property names, narrow activation conditions, and a documented rule for which profiles may be combined. The Maven Help Plugin provides useful diagnostic goals, including mvn help:active-profiles to list active profiles and mvn help:effective-pom to display the effective project model. These commands help explain why a property, dependency, plugin setting, or repository appears in the build. When investigating a profile issue, compare the command line, system properties, environment, POM declarations, parent POM, settings.xml, and active profile list. Be cautious when sharing effective POM output because it can expose internal configuration and potentially sensitive values. A reliable troubleshooting process verifies both the selected profiles and the final configuration Maven constructs, rather than relying only on the profile IDs visible in the POM."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-11-section-9",
        "title": "Maven Profiles — CI/CD Environment Strategy and Secure Release Selection",
        "content": "CI/CD pipelines frequently use Maven profiles to select build variations for testing, packaging, or controlled release operations. A pipeline might run mvn clean verify -Pci for a continuous integration build and use a separately reviewed profile for a specialized packaging requirement. The selected profile should be explicit in the pipeline configuration so that reviewers can identify the intended build behavior. Avoid creating a profile for every deployment environment when the only difference is a runtime endpoint or secret. In many systems, it is safer to build one immutable artifact and inject environment-specific configuration during deployment. This reduces the risk that staging and production receive binaries compiled with different code or dependencies. If profiles genuinely change build output, test each supported profile and record which one produced each artifact. Keep credentials outside the POM and use protected CI variables, secure settings files, or a dedicated secret manager. Restrict production release jobs through approvals and permissions rather than assuming that a profile name provides security. Document the relationship between profile selection, artifact identity, and deployment configuration so that releases remain traceable and reproducible."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-11-section-10",
        "title": "Maven Profiles — Production Diagnostics, Validation, and Hands-on Lab",
        "content": "Consider a pipeline where a developer's local Maven build succeeds, but the CI agent packages the application with a development endpoint. Start by recording the exact Maven command and checking whether a profile was explicitly activated or automatically selected. Run mvn help:active-profiles to identify active profiles, then inspect mvn help:effective-pom to confirm the final property and plugin configuration. Compare the agent's JDK, operating system, system properties, settings.xml, environment variables, and available activation files with the developer machine. If a profile depends on a file, verify that the file exists on the agent and is available at the expected path. If settings profiles or repository credentials are involved, inspect the secure CI configuration without printing secrets. Correct the activation condition or make profile selection explicit in the pipeline. Rebuild the artifact, inspect its packaged configuration, and verify that no development-only endpoint or secret has been included. Run the relevant tests and deployment validation before promoting the artifact. Record the selected profile, build command, artifact checksum, and diagnostic findings. This practical investigation helps distinguish profile activation problems from resource filtering, repository configuration, and runtime configuration mistakes."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "Repositories",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-12-section-1",
        "title": "Maven Repositories — Repository Architecture and Artifact Resolution Flow",
        "content": "Maven repositories are artifact storage locations that provide dependencies, plugins, parent POMs, and other build components required by a project. When Maven encounters a dependency in the project model, it resolves the artifact using its coordinates, which normally include groupId, artifactId, version, and packaging or classifier information where applicable. Maven checks its configured local repository and, when the artifact is unavailable or requires an update, uses the configured remote repository sources according to its repository configuration and resolution rules. A repository normally contains artifacts together with POM files and metadata that help Maven identify available versions and related information. Maven separates the local repository on the build machine from remote repositories accessed over supported transport protocols. Remote repositories may be public services, private company repositories, or repository-manager endpoints. Understanding this resolution flow is essential when diagnosing missing dependencies, unexpected versions, network failures, and inconsistent builds. A successful build on one workstation does not prove that every dependency is available to a clean CI agent. Production build environments should use controlled repository configuration, reliable repository-manager services, and traceable artifact sources."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-12-section-2",
        "title": "Maven Repositories — Local Repository Structure, Caching, and Offline Builds",
        "content": "The Maven local repository stores downloaded artifacts and metadata on the machine or build environment running Maven. Its default location is commonly the .m2/repository directory under the user's home directory, although the location can be changed through Maven settings.xml using the localRepository setting. The directory structure follows artifact coordinates, making it possible to locate group, artifact, and version files. Maven uses this repository to avoid downloading the same artifacts repeatedly, which improves build performance and supports offline execution when all required artifacts are already available. A local repository is a cache, not a reliable organization-wide artifact distribution service. Files may be incomplete after interrupted downloads, removed by cleanup jobs, or populated from different remote sources over time. The -o option requests offline mode, but it succeeds only when the complete dependency and plugin requirements are available locally. CI agents with isolated workspaces may need a configured cache strategy or persistent repository volume. Avoid allowing multiple unrelated processes to modify the same local repository without understanding concurrency and integrity implications. When investigating a suspicious artifact, identify its coordinates and repository origin, then use controlled cache cleanup or a fresh local repository directory to reproduce the resolution process."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-12-section-3",
        "title": "Maven Repositories — Central Repository, Public Repositories, and Custom Endpoints",
        "content": "Maven Central is the primary public repository used by many Maven projects for published libraries and plugins. A project can also declare additional remote repositories when it needs artifacts that are not available from Central or must use an approved organization-specific source. Repository declarations can include an ID, URL, release policy, snapshot policy, and layout information. The repository ID is important because it identifies the repository in Maven configuration and can be associated with server credentials in settings.xml. Custom repository declarations should be added only when there is a clear technical or organizational requirement. Every additional repository increases the number of artifact sources that may influence a build and can introduce availability, security, and supply-chain risks. Prefer an organization-controlled repository manager that proxies approved public sources instead of allowing every project to contact arbitrary external endpoints. Confirm that repository URLs use secure transport and that the service is maintained. When an artifact cannot be resolved, inspect the effective POM and settings configuration to determine which repositories Maven actually sees. Do not assume that a repository mentioned in a dependency's documentation is automatically configured in every consuming project."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-12-section-4",
        "title": "Maven Repositories — Repository Declarations, IDs, and POM Configuration",
        "content": "A project can declare dependency repositories inside the repositories element of its POM. Each repository definition normally includes a unique ID and a URL, with optional policies controlling release and snapshot artifact handling. The ID is not merely a display label: Maven uses it to match repository-specific configuration, including server credentials supplied through settings.xml. A repository declaration may also specify whether releases and snapshots are enabled and how frequently Maven should check for updated metadata. Repository configuration can be inherited through Maven's project model, so a repository declared in a parent POM may affect child projects unless the effective model changes that behavior. Developers should avoid copying multiple similar repository declarations into every module because this makes endpoint changes difficult to maintain. Centralize approved repository definitions in a parent POM or organization settings strategy where appropriate. Validate the final repository list using Maven's effective POM and inspect settings when mirrors are configured. Never place repository passwords or access tokens directly in the POM, because the file is normally committed to source control and distributed to other users."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-12-section-5",
        "title": "Maven Repositories — Mirrors, mirrorOf Rules, and Centralized Routing",
        "content": "Maven mirrors allow an organization or developer to redirect repository requests to another repository endpoint. Mirror configuration is normally declared in settings.xml, which makes it possible to control repository routing without modifying every project's POM. A mirror has an ID, URL, and mirrorOf expression describing which repository IDs it should replace. For example, a company can configure an internal repository manager as a mirror for external repositories so that developers and CI agents retrieve approved artifacts through one controlled service. The mirrorOf value can target a particular repository ID, all repositories using a wildcard, or selected repositories using supported matching expressions and exclusions. Incorrect mirror rules can redirect requests unexpectedly or prevent access to a required repository. A mirror does not itself contain artifacts; it changes the endpoint Maven uses for matching repository requests. When a build behaves differently on a workstation and CI agent, compare their settings.xml files and effective mirror configuration. Validate repository-manager availability, certificate trust, and access permissions before changing mirror rules. Centralized routing improves auditability, caching, and policy enforcement when it is consistently configured across build environments."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-12-section-6",
        "title": "Maven Repositories — Release and Snapshot Policies and Version Selection",
        "content": "Maven repositories can control whether release artifacts and snapshot artifacts are available through separate policies. A release version is intended to represent a published, stable artifact that should not be silently replaced with different content. A snapshot version represents development work that may be updated while the version remains under active development. Repository configuration can enable or disable releases and snapshots independently and can define an update policy that controls when Maven checks remote metadata for newer information. Snapshot resolution may involve timestamped artifacts and metadata that identify the currently published snapshot build. This is different from simply downloading a fixed release file. Teams should publish snapshots to appropriate development repositories and releases to controlled release repositories. Avoid using snapshots as permanent production dependencies because their content may change and reduce build reproducibility. Use immutable release coordinates for production releases and retain artifact history in the repository manager. When a build unexpectedly retrieves an older or newer snapshot, inspect repository policies, metadata, local cache state, and the update policy before assuming that the dependency declaration is incorrect."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-12-section-7",
        "title": "Maven Repositories — Repository Metadata, Checksums, and Artifact Integrity",
        "content": "Repository metadata provides information Maven uses to discover versions, resolve snapshot artifacts, and understand artifact publication state. Metadata files may exist at group, artifact, or version levels depending on their purpose. For example, version-level metadata can help identify the current timestamped snapshot artifact, while artifact-level metadata can describe available versions. Repository managers generate or maintain metadata as artifacts are deployed and updated. Checksums provide a way to verify that downloaded content matches the expected digest supplied by the repository. A checksum mismatch may indicate an incomplete transfer, corrupted cache, inconsistent repository state, or a more serious integrity problem. Maven installations and repository services can differ in how they handle checksum warnings or failures, so production environments should configure and monitor integrity validation deliberately. Do not treat a successful download as proof that an artifact is trustworthy; integrity and provenance are separate concerns. Use trusted repository sources, controlled deployment permissions, and artifact retention policies. When investigating a checksum error, compare the remote artifact and checksum, inspect repository-manager logs, and retry using a clean cache only after confirming the remote content is correct."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-12-section-8",
        "title": "Maven Repositories — Authentication, Server IDs, and Secure Credentials",
        "content": "Private repositories often require authentication before users or CI agents can download dependencies or deploy artifacts. Maven normally keeps repository credentials in settings.xml under the servers element. The server entry ID must match the repository or deployment repository ID that Maven uses, so an ID mismatch can result in authentication failures even when the username and password are correct. Credentials should not be placed in pom.xml because project POM files are commonly committed to source control and shared with other developers. CI systems should inject credentials through protected variables, secret stores, or secured settings files with restricted access. Maven also supports password encryption mechanisms, but encrypted values still require careful protection of the associated master security configuration. Use separate read and deployment permissions where possible so ordinary build jobs cannot publish or overwrite release artifacts. Prefer short-lived credentials or managed identity mechanisms when supported by the repository platform. Do not print settings.xml contents or authentication headers into build logs. When a request returns HTTP 401 or 403, check the server ID, credential injection, token validity, permissions, and repository endpoint before changing dependency coordinates."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-12-section-9",
        "title": "Maven Repositories — Deployment Management and Distribution Management",
        "content": "Downloading dependencies and publishing project artifacts are separate repository operations. Maven uses repository definitions for resolving dependencies, while the distributionManagement section of the POM identifies deployment destinations for artifacts produced by the project. It can define a release repository and a snapshot repository, allowing the build to publish different version types to separate endpoints. Deployment credentials are normally supplied through settings.xml using server IDs that match the configured deployment repository IDs. Publishing is commonly performed through the deploy lifecycle phase when the appropriate Maven Deploy Plugin configuration is available. A deployment job should verify the project version, target repository, credentials, and release approval conditions before publishing. Release repositories should enforce immutability or equivalent governance so that an already published release cannot be silently replaced. Snapshot repositories may permit ongoing updates according to organizational policy. In a multi-module project, validate that all intended modules are deployed and that the resulting metadata is correct. Record the artifact coordinates, build identity, and repository destination so downstream teams can identify exactly which artifact was published."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-12-section-10",
        "title": "Maven Repositories — Nexus, Artifactory, and Repository Manager Architecture",
        "content": "Organizations commonly use repository managers such as Sonatype Nexus Repository or JFrog Artifactory to control artifact access and storage. These platforms can host internally produced artifacts, proxy approved public repositories, and provide group or virtual repositories that combine multiple sources behind a single endpoint. A proxy repository can cache artifacts fetched from an upstream source, reducing repeated external downloads and improving availability during temporary upstream outages. A hosted repository stores artifacts published by the organization, while a group or virtual repository can present multiple repositories through one logical URL. Repository managers also provide access controls, retention policies, audit logs, cleanup tasks, and integration points for software supply-chain governance. Maven clients can be configured to use these endpoints through settings.xml mirrors or project repository configuration. Platform-specific features differ, so administrators must validate the exact repository type and policy behavior in their chosen product. Separate development snapshots from production releases and apply appropriate permissions to each. Monitor storage capacity, upstream connectivity, proxy failures, and service health because repository-manager outages can block otherwise healthy CI pipelines."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-12-section-11",
        "title": "Maven Repositories — Plugin Repositories and Build Tool Resolution",
        "content": "Maven resolves build plugins separately from ordinary project dependencies. Plugin repositories can be configured through pluginRepositories in the POM, while plugin version and execution declarations are normally configured in the build plugins section. This distinction matters when a project can download application libraries but fails to resolve a Maven plugin. A plugin repository definition can specify its ID, URL, release policy, and snapshot policy. Organization-wide mirror settings may redirect plugin repository requests through the same internal repository manager used for dependencies. Build plugins are executable components that run during the build, so their source and version should be controlled just as carefully as application dependencies. Pin plugin versions in the project or parent build configuration rather than relying on implicit version selection. When diagnosing plugin resolution failures, identify the missing plugin coordinates, inspect the effective POM and effective settings, and check whether the plugin repository is reachable and permitted by the mirror rules. Do not assume that adding a dependency repository automatically solves every plugin repository problem. A controlled plugin source improves build stability and reduces the risk of unreviewed build-time code entering the pipeline."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-12-section-12",
        "title": "Maven Repositories — CI/CD Caching, Availability, and Build Reproducibility",
        "content": "Repository configuration has a direct effect on CI/CD build speed, reliability, and reproducibility. A CI agent that downloads every dependency and plugin for every job can waste time and place unnecessary load on external services. A repository manager provides shared caching, while a carefully managed local Maven repository cache can reduce repeated downloads between compatible jobs. Cache keys should account for relevant inputs such as dependency declarations, Maven settings, and toolchain changes so that stale or unsuitable cache contents do not hide real resolution problems. Repository outages can stop builds even when the application source is correct, so production pipelines should monitor repository-manager health and upstream connectivity. Offline builds are useful only when all required artifacts and plugins are already available; they are not a general substitute for repository availability. For reproducibility, pin dependency and plugin versions, use immutable release artifacts, control repository sources, and record the artifact provenance. Avoid allowing a pipeline to resolve different dependency content from uncontrolled public sources on each run. A reliable repository strategy combines caching with source governance rather than treating cache reuse as the only performance solution."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-12-section-13",
        "title": "Maven Repositories — Security Governance, Troubleshooting, and Production Operations",
        "content": "Repository operations require security controls because Maven downloads executable plugins and libraries that become part of the software supply chain. Organizations should restrict which external repositories are permitted, use repository-manager policies to control proxy access, scan artifacts where supported, and separate publishing permissions from read-only dependency access. Repository endpoints should use trusted TLS certificates, and credentials must be stored outside source-controlled POM files. Common failures include HTTP 401 authentication errors, HTTP 403 permission errors, HTTP 404 missing artifacts, connection timeouts, TLS certificate failures, checksum mismatches, and stale snapshot metadata. Begin troubleshooting by capturing the exact Maven command, artifact coordinates, repository URL, and relevant error message. Inspect effective POM and settings configuration, verify mirror rules and server IDs, test endpoint reachability, and review repository-manager logs. Use Maven debug output selectively because verbose logs may expose internal paths or configuration. Do not disable certificate validation or integrity checks as a permanent workaround. For a production incident, verify whether the problem affects one artifact, one repository, one agent, or the entire repository service. Restore service through approved configuration or repository repair, then rerun the build and record the root cause, affected artifacts, and corrective controls."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "Spring Boot Packaging",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-13-section-1",
        "title": "Spring Boot Packaging — Build Tool Fundamentals",
        "content": "# Spring Boot Packaging — Build Tool Fundamentals\n\n## 1. Explanation and Learning Objective\n\nThis section belongs to **Spring Boot Packaging** in Stage 04: Docker Container Engineering.\n\nThis topic concerns Docker container engineering and its role in packaging, distributing, and operating applications. Docker uses images to define application files and runtime defaults, containers to execute isolated processes, networks to provide connectivity, and volumes to preserve data. The exact implementation must be understood in relation to the topic title, its configuration, host dependencies, security boundaries, and operational lifecycle.\n\nDefine the concept represented by this section and explain the engineering problem it solves. Describe where it appears in a Docker-based application lifecycle and identify the Docker components involved. Explain important terms in beginner-friendly language. Distinguish the responsibility of the Docker client, daemon, image, container, registry, network, or storage component wherever relevant.\n\nAfter completing this section, an engineer should be able to explain the concept in plain English, identify the Docker components involved, perform the relevant operation, inspect the resulting state, and recognize the risks of an incorrect implementation.\n\n## 2. Docker Architecture and Technical Behavior\n\nDocker provides a workflow for building, distributing, and running containerized applications. The Docker CLI communicates with the Docker Engine API. The daemon manages images, containers, networks, and volumes. Containers execute application processes with isolation features provided by the host operating system.\n\nAn image provides the filesystem and runtime configuration used to create a container. A container has its own runtime state and writable layer. Networks provide connectivity, and volumes or bind mounts provide access to persistent data. Registries distribute image content between development, CI, and deployment environments.\n\nFor **Spring Boot Packaging — Build Tool Fundamentals**, identify the relevant component, its inputs, its output, and the state it changes. Explain whether the operation happens during image construction, container startup, application execution, network communication, or data persistence. This distinction helps prevent confusion between an image configuration problem and a runtime failure.\n\n## 3. Implementation Workflow\n\n1. Verify Docker CLI and Engine availability with docker version and docker info.\n2. Confirm the correct Docker context and target environment.\n3. Inspect the image, container, network, volume, or configuration involved.\n4. Review the required ports, filesystem paths, environment variables, permissions, and resource limits.\n5. Apply the Dockerfile, CLI, Compose, or runtime configuration change.\n6. Start or rebuild only the resources required for the operation.\n7. Inspect the resulting state and capture logs or command output.\n8. Run application-level validation rather than relying only on container status.\n9. Record the image tag or digest and relevant configuration for repeatability.\n\nDocker commands can affect host resources and persistent data. Verify the selected context and resource names before removing containers, networks, images, or volumes.\n\n## 4. Practical Docker Commands\n\nThe following examples demonstrate relevant inspection and operational commands. Replace example names and image references with values from the actual environment.\n\n`\bash id=\"dkr401\"\ndocker version\ndocker info\ndocker build --no-cache -t payments-api:1.0 .\ndocker image ls\ndocker image inspect payments-api:1.0\ndocker history payments-api:1.0\n`\n\nUse commands that inspect state before commands that modify or remove resources. Do not expose registry credentials, secret environment variables, or private configuration in terminal recordings and CI logs.\n\n## 5. Dockerfile Implementation Example\n\nA small Java application image can use a multi-stage build:\n\n`dockerfile id=\"dkr402\"\nFROM maven:3.9-eclipse-temurin-17 AS build\n\nWORKDIR /workspace\nCOPY pom.xml .\nCOPY src ./src\n\nRUN mvn -B clean package -DskipTests\n\nFROM eclipse-temurin:17-jre\n\nWORKDIR /app\n\nRUN useradd --system --uid 10001 appuser\n\nCOPY --from=build /workspace/target/*.jar app.jar\n\nUSER 10001\n\nEXPOSE 8080\n\nENTRYPOINT [\"java\", \"-jar\", \"/app/app.jar\"]\n`\n\nThe build stage contains the Maven toolchain and creates the application package. The runtime stage contains only the Java runtime and application artifact. This reduces unnecessary build tooling in the final image. A production implementation should pin approved base-image versions, scan the result, validate the JAR selection, and consider image provenance and patching requirements.\n\n## 6. Production DevOps Scenario\n\nConsider a Java payment service that must run consistently on developer machines, CI agents, and production hosts. The team creates a Dockerfile that builds the application and packages its runtime dependencies into an image.\n\nA Jenkins pipeline checks out a specific source commit, builds the image, runs automated tests, and performs a vulnerability scan. After successful validation, the pipeline publishes the image to a controlled registry. The image is identified by a versioned tag and immutable digest.\n\nThe deployment process retrieves the approved image and starts the service with environment-specific configuration. Persistent transaction data is stored outside the disposable container layer. Network rules expose only the required application ports, and the container runs with restricted privileges.\n\nDuring a production incident, engineers inspect the container state, logs, resource consumption, network connectivity, and image digest. They can identify the exact image version and source revision instead of guessing which build is running.\n\n## 7. Common Mistakes and Their Impact\n\n- Using an unpinned base image can introduce unexpected changes during rebuilds.\n- Copying the entire repository into the build context can increase build time and expose unnecessary files.\n- Embedding credentials in Dockerfile instructions can leave secrets in image layers.\n- Running every application process as root increases the impact of a container compromise.\n- Storing database files only in a container writable layer risks data loss.\n- Publishing the wrong host port can make a healthy application unreachable.\n- Assuming container startup means application readiness can cause dependent services to fail.\n- Using mutable image tags without recording digests weakens deployment traceability.\n- Ignoring resource limits can allow one container to exhaust host capacity.\n- Removing volumes during cleanup can permanently delete persistent application data.\n\n## 8. Troubleshooting and Safe Recovery\n\nStart by identifying the affected resource and capturing its current state. Use docker ps -a to inspect container status, docker logs to inspect application output, and docker inspect to examine configuration and runtime state.\n\nFor image-build failures, review the Dockerfile instruction, build context, .dockerignore rules, base image, and build output. For container startup failures, inspect the entrypoint, command, permissions, environment, and exit code. For connectivity failures, check port publishing, network membership, DNS, routing, and host firewall rules. For storage failures, inspect mounts, ownership, disk capacity, and volume state.\n\nCheck daemon and host logs when container-level evidence does not explain the issue. Preserve logs and important data before removing or recreating resources. After applying a correction, verify both container health and actual application behavior.\n\n## 9. Security and Production Best Practices\n\nUse trusted and maintained base images. Pin versions and record image digests for production releases. Scan images for known vulnerabilities and establish a patching process. Avoid embedding secrets in images or source repositories.\n\nRun application processes as non-root users where possible. Drop unnecessary Linux capabilities, avoid privileged containers, restrict host mounts, and protect access to the Docker daemon socket. Apply network segmentation and expose only required ports.\n\nUse volumes or external storage for important data. Configure resource limits, health checks, logging, and monitoring. Keep development, CI, and production configuration separate. Promote the same tested image between environments rather than rebuilding different images for each deployment target.\n\n## 10. Verification and Completion Criteria\n\nThe operation represented by **Spring Boot Packaging — Build Tool Fundamentals** is complete only when the expected Docker state and application behavior are verified.\n\nCheck that:\n\n- The correct Docker context and host were selected.\n- The intended image, container, network, or volume was used.\n- Configuration values and runtime permissions are correct.\n- The expected container process is running.\n- Application logs do not show unresolved startup failures.\n- Required network paths and service endpoints work.\n- Persistent data remains available after container recreation where required.\n- Resource limits and security settings match the intended policy.\n- The image tag or digest can be traced to the validated build.\n- The operation can be repeated using documented steps.\n\n## 11. Practical Exercise\n\nCreate a small application image using a Dockerfile. Build the image with a versioned tag and run it as a container with an explicitly published application port.\n\nInspect the image and container configuration. Read application logs and verify the service endpoint. Introduce one controlled problem, such as an incorrect port mapping or invalid startup command. Observe the failure and collect evidence using Docker inspection and logs.\n\nCorrect the configuration and recreate the container. Confirm that the application works and explain which configuration change resolved the issue. If the application requires persistent data, verify that the data remains available after replacing the container.\n\n## 12. Interview and Engineering Understanding\n\nAn engineer should be able to explain what **Spring Boot Packaging — Build Tool Fundamentals** means, why Docker uses the relevant capability, which components participate, how the operation works, and how the result is verified.\n\nA strong practical answer describes the initial state, the exact Docker configuration or commands, the resulting image or container behavior, the evidence collected, and the production risks. Avoid simply listing commands. Explain what each command changes, which component performs the work, and how the final application behavior is confirmed."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-13-section-2",
        "title": "Spring Boot Packaging — Project Configuration and Structure",
        "content": "# Spring Boot Packaging — Project Configuration and Structure\n\n## 1. Explanation and Learning Objective\n\nThis section belongs to **Spring Boot Packaging** in Stage 04: Docker Container Engineering.\n\nThis topic concerns Docker container engineering and its role in packaging, distributing, and operating applications. Docker uses images to define application files and runtime defaults, containers to execute isolated processes, networks to provide connectivity, and volumes to preserve data. The exact implementation must be understood in relation to the topic title, its configuration, host dependencies, security boundaries, and operational lifecycle.\n\nDescribe prerequisites and the installation or configuration sequence. Cover supported operating-system requirements, Docker Engine or Docker Desktop differences, service startup, CLI connectivity, user permissions, storage configuration, and network access. Explain how to verify installed versions and daemon health. Include safe upgrade planning, package compatibility, service restart impact, and backup considerations for important Docker data.\n\nAfter completing this section, an engineer should be able to explain the concept in plain English, identify the Docker components involved, perform the relevant operation, inspect the resulting state, and recognize the risks of an incorrect implementation.\n\n## 2. Docker Architecture and Technical Behavior\n\nDocker provides a workflow for building, distributing, and running containerized applications. The Docker CLI communicates with the Docker Engine API. The daemon manages images, containers, networks, and volumes. Containers execute application processes with isolation features provided by the host operating system.\n\nAn image provides the filesystem and runtime configuration used to create a container. A container has its own runtime state and writable layer. Networks provide connectivity, and volumes or bind mounts provide access to persistent data. Registries distribute image content between development, CI, and deployment environments.\n\nFor **Spring Boot Packaging — Project Configuration and Structure**, identify the relevant component, its inputs, its output, and the state it changes. Explain whether the operation happens during image construction, container startup, application execution, network communication, or data persistence. This distinction helps prevent confusion between an image configuration problem and a runtime failure.\n\n## 3. Implementation Workflow\n\n1. Verify Docker CLI and Engine availability with docker version and docker info.\n2. Confirm the correct Docker context and target environment.\n3. Inspect the image, container, network, volume, or configuration involved.\n4. Review the required ports, filesystem paths, environment variables, permissions, and resource limits.\n5. Apply the Dockerfile, CLI, Compose, or runtime configuration change.\n6. Start or rebuild only the resources required for the operation.\n7. Inspect the resulting state and capture logs or command output.\n8. Run application-level validation rather than relying only on container status.\n9. Record the image tag or digest and relevant configuration for repeatability.\n\nDocker commands can affect host resources and persistent data. Verify the selected context and resource names before removing containers, networks, images, or volumes.\n\n## 4. Practical Docker Commands\n\nThe following examples demonstrate relevant inspection and operational commands. Replace example names and image references with values from the actual environment.\n\n`\bash id=\"dkr401\"\ndocker version\ndocker info\ndocker ps -a\ndocker image ls\ndocker network ls\ndocker volume ls\ndocker system df\n`\n\nUse commands that inspect state before commands that modify or remove resources. Do not expose registry credentials, secret environment variables, or private configuration in terminal recordings and CI logs.\n\n## 5. Dockerfile Implementation Example\n\nA small Java application image can use a multi-stage build:\n\n`dockerfile id=\"dkr402\"\nFROM maven:3.9-eclipse-temurin-17 AS build\n\nWORKDIR /workspace\nCOPY pom.xml .\nCOPY src ./src\n\nRUN mvn -B clean package -DskipTests\n\nFROM eclipse-temurin:17-jre\n\nWORKDIR /app\n\nRUN useradd --system --uid 10001 appuser\n\nCOPY --from=build /workspace/target/*.jar app.jar\n\nUSER 10001\n\nEXPOSE 8080\n\nENTRYPOINT [\"java\", \"-jar\", \"/app/app.jar\"]\n`\n\nThe build stage contains the Maven toolchain and creates the application package. The runtime stage contains only the Java runtime and application artifact. This reduces unnecessary build tooling in the final image. A production implementation should pin approved base-image versions, scan the result, validate the JAR selection, and consider image provenance and patching requirements.\n\n## 6. Production DevOps Scenario\n\nConsider a Java payment service that must run consistently on developer machines, CI agents, and production hosts. The team creates a Dockerfile that builds the application and packages its runtime dependencies into an image.\n\nA Jenkins pipeline checks out a specific source commit, builds the image, runs automated tests, and performs a vulnerability scan. After successful validation, the pipeline publishes the image to a controlled registry. The image is identified by a versioned tag and immutable digest.\n\nThe deployment process retrieves the approved image and starts the service with environment-specific configuration. Persistent transaction data is stored outside the disposable container layer. Network rules expose only the required application ports, and the container runs with restricted privileges.\n\nDuring a production incident, engineers inspect the container state, logs, resource consumption, network connectivity, and image digest. They can identify the exact image version and source revision instead of guessing which build is running.\n\n## 7. Common Mistakes and Their Impact\n\n- Using an unpinned base image can introduce unexpected changes during rebuilds.\n- Copying the entire repository into the build context can increase build time and expose unnecessary files.\n- Embedding credentials in Dockerfile instructions can leave secrets in image layers.\n- Running every application process as root increases the impact of a container compromise.\n- Storing database files only in a container writable layer risks data loss.\n- Publishing the wrong host port can make a healthy application unreachable.\n- Assuming container startup means application readiness can cause dependent services to fail.\n- Using mutable image tags without recording digests weakens deployment traceability.\n- Ignoring resource limits can allow one container to exhaust host capacity.\n- Removing volumes during cleanup can permanently delete persistent application data.\n\n## 8. Troubleshooting and Safe Recovery\n\nStart by identifying the affected resource and capturing its current state. Use docker ps -a to inspect container status, docker logs to inspect application output, and docker inspect to examine configuration and runtime state.\n\nFor image-build failures, review the Dockerfile instruction, build context, .dockerignore rules, base image, and build output. For container startup failures, inspect the entrypoint, command, permissions, environment, and exit code. For connectivity failures, check port publishing, network membership, DNS, routing, and host firewall rules. For storage failures, inspect mounts, ownership, disk capacity, and volume state.\n\nCheck daemon and host logs when container-level evidence does not explain the issue. Preserve logs and important data before removing or recreating resources. After applying a correction, verify both container health and actual application behavior.\n\n## 9. Security and Production Best Practices\n\nUse trusted and maintained base images. Pin versions and record image digests for production releases. Scan images for known vulnerabilities and establish a patching process. Avoid embedding secrets in images or source repositories.\n\nRun application processes as non-root users where possible. Drop unnecessary Linux capabilities, avoid privileged containers, restrict host mounts, and protect access to the Docker daemon socket. Apply network segmentation and expose only required ports.\n\nUse volumes or external storage for important data. Configure resource limits, health checks, logging, and monitoring. Keep development, CI, and production configuration separate. Promote the same tested image between environments rather than rebuilding different images for each deployment target.\n\n## 10. Verification and Completion Criteria\n\nThe operation represented by **Spring Boot Packaging — Project Configuration and Structure** is complete only when the expected Docker state and application behavior are verified.\n\nCheck that:\n\n- The correct Docker context and host were selected.\n- The intended image, container, network, or volume was used.\n- Configuration values and runtime permissions are correct.\n- The expected container process is running.\n- Application logs do not show unresolved startup failures.\n- Required network paths and service endpoints work.\n- Persistent data remains available after container recreation where required.\n- Resource limits and security settings match the intended policy.\n- The image tag or digest can be traced to the validated build.\n- The operation can be repeated using documented steps.\n\n## 11. Practical Exercise\n\nCreate a small application image using a Dockerfile. Build the image with a versioned tag and run it as a container with an explicitly published application port.\n\nInspect the image and container configuration. Read application logs and verify the service endpoint. Introduce one controlled problem, such as an incorrect port mapping or invalid startup command. Observe the failure and collect evidence using Docker inspection and logs.\n\nCorrect the configuration and recreate the container. Confirm that the application works and explain which configuration change resolved the issue. If the application requires persistent data, verify that the data remains available after replacing the container.\n\n## 12. Interview and Engineering Understanding\n\nAn engineer should be able to explain what **Spring Boot Packaging — Project Configuration and Structure** means, why Docker uses the relevant capability, which components participate, how the operation works, and how the result is verified.\n\nA strong practical answer describes the initial state, the exact Docker configuration or commands, the resulting image or container behavior, the evidence collected, and the production risks. Avoid simply listing commands. Explain what each command changes, which component performs the work, and how the final application behavior is confirmed."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-13-section-3",
        "title": "Spring Boot Packaging — Dependency Resolution",
        "content": "# Spring Boot Packaging — Dependency Resolution\n\n## 1. Explanation and Learning Objective\n\nThis section belongs to **Spring Boot Packaging** in Stage 04: Docker Container Engineering.\n\nThis topic concerns Docker container engineering and its role in packaging, distributing, and operating applications. Docker uses images to define application files and runtime defaults, containers to execute isolated processes, networks to provide connectivity, and volumes to preserve data. The exact implementation must be understood in relation to the topic title, its configuration, host dependencies, security boundaries, and operational lifecycle.\n\nExplain the specific Docker engineering responsibility represented by this section title. Define its purpose, identify the Docker components involved, describe the operation sequence, and explain the expected result. Include relevant Dockerfile, CLI, Compose, daemon, or runtime configuration as applicable. Explain common failure modes, evidence collection, safe recovery, security considerations, and production verification.\n\nAfter completing this section, an engineer should be able to explain the concept in plain English, identify the Docker components involved, perform the relevant operation, inspect the resulting state, and recognize the risks of an incorrect implementation.\n\n## 2. Docker Architecture and Technical Behavior\n\nDocker provides a workflow for building, distributing, and running containerized applications. The Docker CLI communicates with the Docker Engine API. The daemon manages images, containers, networks, and volumes. Containers execute application processes with isolation features provided by the host operating system.\n\nAn image provides the filesystem and runtime configuration used to create a container. A container has its own runtime state and writable layer. Networks provide connectivity, and volumes or bind mounts provide access to persistent data. Registries distribute image content between development, CI, and deployment environments.\n\nFor **Spring Boot Packaging — Dependency Resolution**, identify the relevant component, its inputs, its output, and the state it changes. Explain whether the operation happens during image construction, container startup, application execution, network communication, or data persistence. This distinction helps prevent confusion between an image configuration problem and a runtime failure.\n\n## 3. Implementation Workflow\n\n1. Verify Docker CLI and Engine availability with docker version and docker info.\n2. Confirm the correct Docker context and target environment.\n3. Inspect the image, container, network, volume, or configuration involved.\n4. Review the required ports, filesystem paths, environment variables, permissions, and resource limits.\n5. Apply the Dockerfile, CLI, Compose, or runtime configuration change.\n6. Start or rebuild only the resources required for the operation.\n7. Inspect the resulting state and capture logs or command output.\n8. Run application-level validation rather than relying only on container status.\n9. Record the image tag or digest and relevant configuration for repeatability.\n\nDocker commands can affect host resources and persistent data. Verify the selected context and resource names before removing containers, networks, images, or volumes.\n\n## 4. Practical Docker Commands\n\nThe following examples demonstrate relevant inspection and operational commands. Replace example names and image references with values from the actual environment.\n\n`\bash id=\"dkr401\"\ndocker version\ndocker info\ndocker ps -a\ndocker image ls\ndocker network ls\ndocker volume ls\ndocker system df\n`\n\nUse commands that inspect state before commands that modify or remove resources. Do not expose registry credentials, secret environment variables, or private configuration in terminal recordings and CI logs.\n\n## 5. Dockerfile Implementation Example\n\nA small Java application image can