      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-14-section-7",
        "title": "Surefire and Failsafe — Repository and Artifact Management",
        "content": "# Surefire and Failsafe — Repository and Artifact Management\n\n## 1. Explanation and Learning Objective\n\nThis section belongs to **Surefire and Failsafe** in Stage 04: Docker Container Engineering.\n\nThis topic concerns Docker container engineering and its role in packaging, distributing, and operating applications. Docker uses images to define application files and runtime defaults, containers to execute isolated processes, networks to provide connectivity, and volumes to preserve data. The exact implementation must be understood in relation to the topic title, its configuration, host dependencies, security boundaries, and operational lifecycle.\n\nExplain how Docker images are tagged, stored, retrieved, and distributed through registries. Cover image naming, registry hostnames, namespaces, tags, manifests, layers, digests, authentication, and push or pull behavior. Explain why mutable tags such as latest are not sufficient for reliable production traceability and why image digests provide immutable content identity. Include access control and credential protection.\n\nAfter completing this section, an engineer should be able to explain the concept in plain English, identify the Docker components involved, perform the relevant operation, inspect the resulting state, and recognize the risks of an incorrect implementation.\n\n## 2. Docker Architecture and Technical Behavior\n\nDocker provides a workflow for building, distributing, and running containerized applications. The Docker CLI communicates with the Docker Engine API. The daemon manages images, containers, networks, and volumes. Containers execute application processes with isolation features provided by the host operating system.\n\nAn image provides the filesystem and runtime configuration used to create a container. A container has its own runtime state and writable layer. Networks provide connectivity, and volumes or bind mounts provide access to persistent data. Registries distribute image content between development, CI, and deployment environments.\n\nFor **Surefire and Failsafe — Repository and Artifact Management**, identify the relevant component, its inputs, its output, and the state it changes. Explain whether the operation happens during image construction, container startup, application execution, network communication, or data persistence. This distinction helps prevent confusion between an image configuration problem and a runtime failure.\n\n## 3. Implementation Workflow\n\n1. Verify Docker CLI and Engine availability with docker version and docker info.\n2. Confirm the correct Docker context and target environment.\n3. Inspect the image, container, network, volume, or configuration involved.\n4. Review the required ports, filesystem paths, environment variables, permissions, and resource limits.\n5. Apply the Dockerfile, CLI, Compose, or runtime configuration change.\n6. Start or rebuild only the resources required for the operation.\n7. Inspect the resulting state and capture logs or command output.\n8. Run application-level validation rather than relying only on container status.\n9. Record the image tag or digest and relevant configuration for repeatability.\n\nDocker commands can affect host resources and persistent data. Verify the selected context and resource names before removing containers, networks, images, or volumes.\n\n## 4. Practical Docker Commands\n\nThe following examples demonstrate relevant inspection and operational commands. Replace example names and image references with values from the actual environment.\n\n`\bash id=\"dkr401\"\ndocker login registry.example.com\ndocker tag payments-api:1.0 registry.example.com/team/payments-api:1.0\ndocker push registry.example.com/team/payments-api:1.0\ndocker pull registry.example.com/team/payments-api:1.0\ndocker image inspect registry.example.com/team/payments-api:1.0\n`\n\nUse commands that inspect state before commands that modify or remove resources. Do not expose registry credentials, secret environment variables, or private configuration in terminal recordings and CI logs.\n\n## 5. Dockerfile Implementation Example\n\nA small Java application image can use a multi-stage build:\n\n`dockerfile id=\"dkr402\"\nFROM maven:3.9-eclipse-temurin-17 AS build\n\nWORKDIR /workspace\nCOPY pom.xml .\nCOPY src ./src\n\nRUN mvn -B clean package -DskipTests\n\nFROM eclipse-temurin:17-jre\n\nWORKDIR /app\n\nRUN useradd --system --uid 10001 appuser\n\nCOPY --from=build /workspace/target/*.jar app.jar\n\nUSER 10001\n\nEXPOSE 8080\n\nENTRYPOINT [\"java\", \"-jar\", \"/app/app.jar\"]\n`\n\nThe build stage contains the Maven toolchain and creates the application package. The runtime stage contains only the Java runtime and application artifact. This reduces unnecessary build tooling in the final image. A production implementation should pin approved base-image versions, scan the result, validate the JAR selection, and consider image provenance and patching requirements.\n\n## 6. Production DevOps Scenario\n\nConsider a Java payment service that must run consistently on developer machines, CI agents, and production hosts. The team creates a Dockerfile that builds the application and packages its runtime dependencies into an image.\n\nA Jenkins pipeline checks out a specific source commit, builds the image, runs automated tests, and performs a vulnerability scan. After successful validation, the pipeline publishes the image to a controlled registry. The image is identified by a versioned tag and immutable digest.\n\nThe deployment process retrieves the approved image and starts the service with environment-specific configuration. Persistent transaction data is stored outside the disposable container layer. Network rules expose only the required application ports, and the container runs with restricted privileges.\n\nDuring a production incident, engineers inspect the container state, logs, resource consumption, network connectivity, and image digest. They can identify the exact image version and source revision instead of guessing which build is running.\n\n## 7. Common Mistakes and Their Impact\n\n- Using an unpinned base image can introduce unexpected changes during rebuilds.\n- Copying the entire repository into the build context can increase build time and expose unnecessary files.\n- Embedding credentials in Dockerfile instructions can leave secrets in image layers.\n- Running every application process as root increases the impact of a container compromise.\n- Storing database files only in a container writable layer risks data loss.\n- Publishing the wrong host port can make a healthy application unreachable.\n- Assuming container startup means application readiness can cause dependent services to fail.\n- Using mutable image tags without recording digests weakens deployment traceability.\n- Ignoring resource limits can allow one container to exhaust host capacity.\n- Removing volumes during cleanup can permanently delete persistent application data.\n\n## 8. Troubleshooting and Safe Recovery\n\nStart by identifying the affected resource and capturing its current state. Use docker ps -a to inspect container status, docker logs to inspect application output, and docker inspect to examine configuration and runtime state.\n\nFor image-build failures, review the Dockerfile instruction, build context, .dockerignore rules, base image, and build output. For container startup failures, inspect the entrypoint, command, permissions, environment, and exit code. For connectivity failures, check port publishing, network membership, DNS, routing, and host firewall rules. For storage failures, inspect mounts, ownership, disk capacity, and volume state.\n\nCheck daemon and host logs when container-level evidence does not explain the issue. Preserve logs and important data before removing or recreating resources. After applying a correction, verify both container health and actual application behavior.\n\n## 9. Security and Production Best Practices\n\nUse trusted and maintained base images. Pin versions and record image digests for production releases. Scan images for known vulnerabilities and establish a patching process. Avoid embedding secrets in images or source repositories.\n\nRun application processes as non-root users where possible. Drop unnecessary Linux capabilities, avoid privileged containers, restrict host mounts, and protect access to the Docker daemon socket. Apply network segmentation and expose only required ports.\n\nUse volumes or external storage for important data. Configure resource limits, health checks, logging, and monitoring. Keep development, CI, and production configuration separate. Promote the same tested image between environments rather than rebuilding different images for each deployment target.\n\n## 10. Verification and Completion Criteria\n\nThe operation represented by **Surefire and Failsafe — Repository and Artifact Management** is complete only when the expected Docker state and application behavior are verified.\n\nCheck that:\n\n- The correct Docker context and host were selected.\n- The intended image, container, network, or volume was used.\n- Configuration values and runtime permissions are correct.\n- The expected container process is running.\n- Application logs do not show unresolved startup failures.\n- Required network paths and service endpoints work.\n- Persistent data remains available after container recreation where required.\n- Resource limits and security settings match the intended policy.\n- The image tag or digest can be traced to the validated build.\n- The operation can be repeated using documented steps.\n\n## 11. Practical Exercise\n\nCreate a small application image using a Dockerfile. Build the image with a versioned tag and run it as a container with an explicitly published application port.\n\nInspect the image and container configuration. Read application logs and verify the service endpoint. Introduce one controlled problem, such as an incorrect port mapping or invalid startup command. Observe the failure and collect evidence using Docker inspection and logs.\n\nCorrect the configuration and recreate the container. Confirm that the application works and explain which configuration change resolved the issue. If the application requires persistent data, verify that the data remains available after replacing the container.\n\n## 12. Interview and Engineering Understanding\n\nAn engineer should be able to explain what **Surefire and Failsafe — Repository and Artifact Management** means, why Docker uses the relevant capability, which components participate, how the operation works, and how the result is verified.\n\nA strong practical answer describes the initial state, the exact Docker configuration or commands, the resulting image or container behavior, the evidence collected, and the production risks. Avoid simply listing commands. Explain what each command changes, which component performs the work, and how the final application behavior is confirmed."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-14-section-8",
        "title": "Surefire and Failsafe — Testing and Packaging",
        "content": "# Surefire and Failsafe — Testing and Packaging\n\n## 1. Explanation and Learning Objective\n\nThis section belongs to **Surefire and Failsafe** in Stage 04: Docker Container Engineering.\n\nThis topic concerns Docker container engineering and its role in packaging, distributing, and operating applications. Docker uses images to define application files and runtime defaults, containers to execute isolated processes, networks to provide connectivity, and volumes to preserve data. The exact implementation must be understood in relation to the topic title, its configuration, host dependencies, security boundaries, and operational lifecycle.\n\nDescribe a reproducible practical exercise for this section. State the host prerequisites, Docker resources required, exact configuration or commands, expected intermediate state, and final result. Include a successful execution and a controlled failure case. Verify the outcome through docker inspect, docker logs, docker image ls, docker network inspect, docker volume inspect, container statistics, or the relevant Compose output.\n\nAfter completing this section, an engineer should be able to explain the concept in plain English, identify the Docker components involved, perform the relevant operation, inspect the resulting state, and recognize the risks of an incorrect implementation.\n\n## 2. Docker Architecture and Technical Behavior\n\nDocker provides a workflow for building, distributing, and running containerized applications. The Docker CLI communicates with the Docker Engine API. The daemon manages images, containers, networks, and volumes. Containers execute application processes with isolation features provided by the host operating system.\n\nAn image provides the filesystem and runtime configuration used to create a container. A container has its own runtime state and writable layer. Networks provide connectivity, and volumes or bind mounts provide access to persistent data. Registries distribute image content between development, CI, and deployment environments.\n\nFor **Surefire and Failsafe — Testing and Packaging**, identify the relevant component, its inputs, its output, and the state it changes. Explain whether the operation happens during image construction, container startup, application execution, network communication, or data persistence. This distinction helps prevent confusion between an image configuration problem and a runtime failure.\n\n## 3. Implementation Workflow\n\n1. Verify Docker CLI and Engine availability with docker version and docker info.\n2. Confirm the correct Docker context and target environment.\n3. Inspect the image, container, network, volume, or configuration involved.\n4. Review the required ports, filesystem paths, environment variables, permissions, and resource limits.\n5. Apply the Dockerfile, CLI, Compose, or runtime configuration change.\n6. Start or rebuild only the resources required for the operation.\n7. Inspect the resulting state and capture logs or command output.\n8. Run application-level validation rather than relying only on container status.\n9. Record the image tag or digest and relevant configuration for repeatability.\n\nDocker commands can affect host resources and persistent data. Verify the selected context and resource names before removing containers, networks, images, or volumes.\n\n## 4. Practical Docker Commands\n\nThe following examples demonstrate relevant inspection and operational commands. Replace example names and image references with values from the actual environment.\n\n`\bash id=\"dkr401\"\ndocker version\ndocker info\ndocker ps -a\ndocker image ls\ndocker network ls\ndocker volume ls\ndocker system df\n`\n\nUse commands that inspect state before commands that modify or remove resources. Do not expose registry credentials, secret environment variables, or private configuration in terminal recordings and CI logs.\n\n## 5. Dockerfile Implementation Example\n\nA small Java application image can use a multi-stage build:\n\n`dockerfile id=\"dkr402\"\nFROM maven:3.9-eclipse-temurin-17 AS build\n\nWORKDIR /workspace\nCOPY pom.xml .\nCOPY src ./src\n\nRUN mvn -B clean package -DskipTests\n\nFROM eclipse-temurin:17-jre\n\nWORKDIR /app\n\nRUN useradd --system --uid 10001 appuser\n\nCOPY --from=build /workspace/target/*.jar app.jar\n\nUSER 10001\n\nEXPOSE 8080\n\nENTRYPOINT [\"java\", \"-jar\", \"/app/app.jar\"]\n`\n\nThe build stage contains the Maven toolchain and creates the application package. The runtime stage contains only the Java runtime and application artifact. This reduces unnecessary build tooling in the final image. A production implementation should pin approved base-image versions, scan the result, validate the JAR selection, and consider image provenance and patching requirements.\n\n## 6. Production DevOps Scenario\n\nConsider a Java payment service that must run consistently on developer machines, CI agents, and production hosts. The team creates a Dockerfile that builds the application and packages its runtime dependencies into an image.\n\nA Jenkins pipeline checks out a specific source commit, builds the image, runs automated tests, and performs a vulnerability scan. After successful validation, the pipeline publishes the image to a controlled registry. The image is identified by a versioned tag and immutable digest.\n\nThe deployment process retrieves the approved image and starts the service with environment-specific configuration. Persistent transaction data is stored outside the disposable container layer. Network rules expose only the required application ports, and the container runs with restricted privileges.\n\nDuring a production incident, engineers inspect the container state, logs, resource consumption, network connectivity, and image digest. They can identify the exact image version and source revision instead of guessing which build is running.\n\n## 7. Common Mistakes and Their Impact\n\n- Using an unpinned base image can introduce unexpected changes during rebuilds.\n- Copying the entire repository into the build context can increase build time and expose unnecessary files.\n- Embedding credentials in Dockerfile instructions can leave secrets in image layers.\n- Running every application process as root increases the impact of a container compromise.\n- Storing database files only in a container writable layer risks data loss.\n- Publishing the wrong host port can make a healthy application unreachable.\n- Assuming container startup means application readiness can cause dependent services to fail.\n- Using mutable image tags without recording digests weakens deployment traceability.\n- Ignoring resource limits can allow one container to exhaust host capacity.\n- Removing volumes during cleanup can permanently delete persistent application data.\n\n## 8. Troubleshooting and Safe Recovery\n\nStart by identifying the affected resource and capturing its current state. Use docker ps -a to inspect container status, docker logs to inspect application output, and docker inspect to examine configuration and runtime state.\n\nFor image-build failures, review the Dockerfile instruction, build context, .dockerignore rules, base image, and build output. For container startup failures, inspect the entrypoint, command, permissions, environment, and exit code. For connectivity failures, check port publishing, network membership, DNS, routing, and host firewall rules. For storage failures, inspect mounts, ownership, disk capacity, and volume state.\n\nCheck daemon and host logs when container-level evidence does not explain the issue. Preserve logs and important data before removing or recreating resources. After applying a correction, verify both container health and actual application behavior.\n\n## 9. Security and Production Best Practices\n\nUse trusted and maintained base images. Pin versions and record image digests for production releases. Scan images for known vulnerabilities and establish a patching process. Avoid embedding secrets in images or source repositories.\n\nRun application processes as non-root users where possible. Drop unnecessary Linux capabilities, avoid privileged containers, restrict host mounts, and protect access to the Docker daemon socket. Apply network segmentation and expose only required ports.\n\nUse volumes or external storage for important data. Configure resource limits, health checks, logging, and monitoring. Keep development, CI, and production configuration separate. Promote the same tested image between environments rather than rebuilding different images for each deployment target.\n\n## 10. Verification and Completion Criteria\n\nThe operation represented by **Surefire and Failsafe — Testing and Packaging** is complete only when the expected Docker state and application behavior are verified.\n\nCheck that:\n\n- The correct Docker context and host were selected.\n- The intended image, container, network, or volume was used.\n- Configuration values and runtime permissions are correct.\n- The expected container process is running.\n- Application logs do not show unresolved startup failures.\n- Required network paths and service endpoints work.\n- Persistent data remains available after container recreation where required.\n- Resource limits and security settings match the intended policy.\n- The image tag or digest can be traced to the validated build.\n- The operation can be repeated using documented steps.\n\n## 11. Practical Exercise\n\nCreate a small application image using a Dockerfile. Build the image with a versioned tag and run it as a container with an explicitly published application port.\n\nInspect the image and container configuration. Read application logs and verify the service endpoint. Introduce one controlled problem, such as an incorrect port mapping or invalid startup command. Observe the failure and collect evidence using Docker inspection and logs.\n\nCorrect the configuration and recreate the container. Confirm that the application works and explain which configuration change resolved the issue. If the application requires persistent data, verify that the data remains available after replacing the container.\n\n## 12. Interview and Engineering Understanding\n\nAn engineer should be able to explain what **Surefire and Failsafe — Testing and Packaging** means, why Docker uses the relevant capability, which components participate, how the operation works, and how the result is verified.\n\nA strong practical answer describes the initial state, the exact Docker configuration or commands, the resulting image or container behavior, the evidence collected, and the production risks. Avoid simply listing commands. Explain what each command changes, which component performs the work, and how the final application behavior is confirmed."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-4-topic-14-section-9",
        "title": "Surefire and Failsafe — Build Reproducibility",
        "content": "# Surefire and Failsafe — Build Reproducibility\n\n## 1. Explanation and Learning Objective\n\nThis section belongs to **Surefire and Failsafe** in Stage 04: Docker Container Engineering.\n\nThis topic concerns Docker container engineering and its role in packaging, distributing, and operating applications. Docker uses images to define application files and runtime defaults, containers to execute isolated processes, networks to provide connectivity, and volumes to preserve data. The exact implementation must be understood in relation to the topic title, its configuration, host dependencies, security boundaries, and operational lifecycle.\n\nExplain the image build process and how Dockerfile instructions contribute to the resulting image. Cover FROM, WORKDIR, COPY, ADD where appropriate, RUN, ENV, ARG, EXPOSE, USER, CMD, and ENTRYPOINT according to the section's scope. Explain build context, .dockerignore, layer caching, multi-stage builds, reproducibility, image size, and the distinction between build-time arguments and runtime environment variables. Include the security risks of embedding secrets in image layers.\n\nAfter completing this section, an engineer should be able to explain the concept in plain English, identify the Docker components involved, perform the relevant operation, inspect the resulting state, and recognize the risks of an incorrect implementation.\n\n## 2. Docker Architecture and Technical Behavior\n\nDocker provides a workflow for building, distributing, and running containerized applications. The Docker CLI communicates with the Docker Engine API. The daemon manages images, containers, networks, and volumes. Containers execute application processes with isolation features provided by the host operating system.\n\nAn image provides the filesystem and runtime configuration used to create a container. A container has its own runtime state and writable layer. Networks provide connectivity, and volumes or bind mounts provide access to persistent data. Registries distribute image content between development, CI, and deployment environments.\n\nFor **Surefire and Failsafe — Build Reproducibility**, identify the relevant component, its inputs, its output, and the state it changes. Explain whether the operation happens during image construction, container startup, application execution, network communication, or data persistence. This distinction helps prevent confusion between an image configuration problem and a runtime failure.\n\n## 3. Implementation Workflow\n\n1. Verify Docker CLI and Engine availability with docker version and docker info.\n2. Confirm the correct Docker context and target environment.\n3. Inspect the image, container, network, volume, or configuration involved.\n4. Review the required ports, filesystem paths, environment variables, permissions, and resource limits.\n5. Apply the Dockerfile, CLI, Compose, or runtime configuration change.\n6. Start or rebuild only the resources required for the operation.\n7. Inspect the resulting state and capture logs or command output.\n8. Run application-level validation rather than relying only on container status.\n9. Record the image tag or digest and relevant configuration for repeatability.\n\nDocker commands can affect host resources and persistent data. Verify the selected context and resource names before removing containers, networks, images, or volumes.\n\n## 4. Practical Docker Commands\n\nThe following examples demonstrate relevant inspection and operational commands. Replace example names and image references with values from the actual environment.\n\n`\bash id=\"dkr401\"\ndocker version\ndocker info\ndocker build --no-cache -t payments-api:1.0 .\ndocker image ls\ndocker image inspect payments-api:1.0\ndocker history payments-api:1.0\n`\n\nUse commands that inspect state before commands that modify or remove resources. Do not expose registry credentials, secret environment variables, or private configuration in terminal recordings and CI logs.\n\n## 5. Dockerfile Implementation Example\n\nA small Java application image can use a multi-stage build:\n\n`dockerfile id=\"dkr402\"\nFROM maven:3.9-eclipse-temurin-17 AS build\n\nWORKDIR /workspace\nCOPY pom.xml .\nCOPY src ./src\n\nRUN mvn -B clean package -DskipTests\n\nFROM eclipse-temurin:17-jre\n\nWORKDIR /app\n\nRUN useradd --system --uid 10001 appuser\n\nCOPY --from=build /workspace/target/*.jar app.jar\n\nUSER 10001\n\nEXPOSE 8080\n\nENTRYPOINT [\"java\", \"-jar\", \"/app/app.jar\"]\n`\n\nThe build stage contains the Maven toolchain and creates the application package. The runtime stage contains only the Java runtime and application artifact. This reduces unnecessary build tooling in the final image. A production implementation should pin approved base-image versions, scan the result, validate the JAR selection, and consider image provenance and patching requirements.\n\n## 6. Production DevOps Scenario\n\nConsider a Java payment service that must run consistently on developer machines, CI agents, and production hosts. The team creates a Dockerfile that builds the application and packages its runtime dependencies into an image.\n\nA Jenkins pipeline checks out a specific source commit, builds the image, runs automated tests, and performs a vulnerability scan. After successful validation, the pipeline publishes the image to a controlled registry. The image is identified by a versioned tag and immutable digest.\n\nThe deployment process retrieves the approved image and starts the service with environment-specific configuration. Persistent transaction data is stored outside the disposable container layer. Network rules expose only the required application ports, and the container runs with restricted privileges.\n\nDuring a production incident, engineers inspect the container state, logs, resource consumption, network connectivity, and image digest. They can identify the exact image version and source revision instead of guessing which build is running.\n\n## 7. Common Mistakes and Their Impact\n\n- Using an unpinned base image can introduce unexpected changes during rebuilds.\n- Copying the entire repository into the build context can increase build time and expose unnecessary files.\n- Embedding credentials in Dockerfile instructions can leave secrets in image layers.\n- Running every application process as root increases the impact of a container compromise.\n- Storing database files only in a container writable layer risks data loss.\n- Publishing the wrong host port can make a healthy application unreachable.\n- Assuming container startup means application readiness can cause dependent services to fail.\n- Using mutable image tags without recording digests weakens deployment traceability.\n- Ignoring resource limits can allow one container to exhaust host capacity.\n- Removing volumes during cleanup can permanently delete persistent application data.\n\n## 8. Troubleshooting and Safe Recovery\n\nStart by identifying the affected resource and capturing its current state. Use docker ps -a to inspect container status, docker logs to inspect application output, and docker inspect to examine configuration and runtime state.\n\nFor image-build failures, review the Dockerfile instruction, build context, .dockerignore rules, base image, and build output. For container startup failures, inspect the entrypoint, command, permissions, environment, and exit code. For connectivity failures, check port publishing, network membership, DNS, routing, and host firewall rules. For storage failures, inspect mounts, ownership, disk capacity, and volume state.\n\nCheck daemon and host logs when container-level evidence does not explain the issue. Preserve logs and important data before removing or recreating resources. After applying a correction, verify both container health and actual application behavior.\n\n## 9. Security and Production Best Practices\n\nUse trusted and maintained base images. Pin versions and record image digests for production releases. Scan images for known vulnerabilities and establish a patching process. Avoid embedding secrets in images or source repositories.\n\nRun application processes as non-root users where possible. Drop unnecessary Linux capabilities, avoid privileged containers, restrict host mounts, and protect access to the Docker daemon socket. Apply network segmentation and expose only required ports.\n\nUse volumes or external storage for important data. Configure resource limits, health checks, logging, and monitoring. Keep development, CI, and production configuration separate. Promote the same tested image between environments rather than rebuilding different images for each deployment target.\n\n## 10. Verification and Completion Criteria\n\nThe operation represented by **Surefire and Failsafe — Build Reproducibility** is complete only when the expected Docker state and application behavior are verified.\n\nCheck that:\n\n- The correct Docker context and host were selected.\n- The intended image, container, network, or volume was used.\n- Configuration values and runtime permissions are correct.\n- The expected container process is running.\n- Application logs do not show unresolved startup failures.\n- Required network paths and service endpoints work.\n- Persistent data remains available after container recreation where required.\n- Resource limits and security settings match the intended policy.\n- The image tag or digest can be traced to the validated build.\n- The operation can be repeated using documented steps.\n\n## 11. Practical Exercise\n\nCreate a small application image using a Dockerfile. Build the image with a versioned tag and run it as a container with an explicitly published application port.\n\nInspect the image and container configuration. Read application logs and verify the service endpoint. Introduce one controlled problem, such as an incorrect port mapping or invalid startup command. Observe the failure and collect evidence using Docker inspection and logs.\n\nCorrect the configuration and recreate the container. Confirm that the application works and explain which configuration change resolved the issue. If the application requires persistent data, verify that the data remains available after replacing the container.\n\n## 12. Interview and Engineering Understanding\n\nAn engineer should be able to explain what **Surefire and Failsafe — Build Reproducibility** means, why Docker uses the relevant capability, which components participate, how the operation works, and how the result is verified.\n\nA strong practical answer describes the initial state, the exact Docker configuration or commands, the resulting image or container behavior, the evidence collected, and the production risks. Avoid simply listing commands. Explain what each command changes, which component performs the work, and how the final application behavior is confirmed."
